import type { ArticleCore } from "../types";

export const CORES_D: Record<string, ArticleCore> = {
  "mind": {
    lede: `Mind is a continent of the atlas rather than a substance hidden behind the eyes. It names the inner workshop in which [[perception|Perception]] builds a world, [[memory|Memory]] rebuilds a past, [[language|Language]] makes thought public, and [[decision-making|Decision Making]] spends a scarce store of [[attention|Attention]] on what to do next. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] supplies the experiments; [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] insists the workshop is never empty of other people; [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] offers the wet circuitry. [[consciousness|Consciousness]] remains the unsolved glow at the rim. The hub exists so a reader can enter from a neuron, a sentence, a bias, or a feeling and still be in the same country.`,
    idea: `To treat mind as a continent is to refuse two older maps at once: the ghost that floats free of the body, and the machine that has no interior. What lives here is a set of capacities — seeing, holding, saying, choosing, feeling — that can be studied without first settling what a mind is made of. [[working-memory|Working memory]] is the expensive desk on which those capacities actually operate. [[learning|Learning]] is how the desk is rebuilt by use. [[emotion|Emotion]] colors the work so that not every option is equal. The [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]] offers one unifying picture, in which the organism is always predicting; [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] offers another, in which prediction is a skill of a body in a world. Neither picture owns the hub. The atlas files mind beside [[philosophy|Philosophy]] and [[biology|Biology]] because the questions leak: a theory of knowing becomes a theory of nervous tissue, then a theory of [[consciousness|Consciousness]], then a method for building [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]]. A reader who wants a single reduction will be disappointed. A reader who wants a neighborhood of mutually constraining accounts will be at home.`,
    lineage: `The word mind is older than the laboratories that now claim it. Greek psyche, Latin mens, and the early-modern soul all named an interior that [[philosophy|Philosophy]] could argue about without opening a skull. [[james|William James]], writing in 1890, still had to invent a science while sharing the page with metaphysics; his stream of [[consciousness|Consciousness]] is a hinge between those worlds. Behaviorism then tried to do without the interior altogether, and for a generation the respectable object was the reflex. The mid-century cognitive turn — fed by [[turing|Alan Turing]]'s machines, by information theory, and by [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]]'s attack on stimulus-response linguistics — put representations back on the table. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] became the experimental weather of the continent; [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] later supplied imaging and cells. [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] had already shown that remembering is reconstructing; [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]] would show that deciding is not the same as maximizing. The hub gathers those turns so they can be read as one landscape rather than as departmental debris.`,
    tension: `The standing quarrel of this continent is whether mind is a natural kind or a useful heap. Some neighbors pull toward [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] and want every verb — perceive, recall, choose — cashed out in circuits. Others pull toward [[phenomenology|Phenomenology]] and [[consciousness|Consciousness]] and insist that the cashing-out leaves the thing itself behind. [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] splits the mind in two and is accused of splitting it too cleanly. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] refuses the split between program and body and is accused of dissolving mechanism into metaphor. Meanwhile [[language|Language]] and [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] keep showing that a solitary mind is already a crowd. The atlas does not pick a winner. It keeps the fault visible, because the strain is what still produces experiments, therapies, and machines.`,
  },
  "cognitive-psychology": {
    lede: `[[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] is the experimental science of mental process — the laboratory descendant of questions that once belonged only to [[philosophy|Philosophy]]. It asks how a mind sees a scene, holds a number, parses a sentence, and chooses a door, and it insists those hows can be measured. Against the behaviorist ban on inner life, the cognitive revolution of the mid-twentieth century put representations and procedures back on the table. [[memory|Memory]], [[attention|Attention]], [[perception|Perception]], and [[decision-making|Decision Making]] are not chapter headings here; they are paradigms with error bars. The field sits beside [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] as a sibling, sometimes a rival, always a source of models.`,
    idea: `The working unit of cognitive psychology is not the soul and not the neuron but the process: encoding, storage, retrieval, selection, inference. A typical experiment isolates one of those verbs, times it, loads it, and watches it fail. From the failures come architectures. [[working-memory|Working memory]] is capacity under load; [[attention|Attention]] is selection under competition; [[perception|Perception]] is construction under ambiguity. The [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]] recasts many of those processes as probabilistic prediction, while [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] sorts them into fast and slow. Neither is a replacement for the experiment; both are bets about what the experiment is measuring. The field also talks constantly to neighboring machines. [[neural-networks|Neural networks]] and [[machine-learning|Machine Learning]] now supply both metaphors and methods, and [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] supplies constraints that a purely functional model can ignore only for a while. What distinguishes the topic from its neighbors is a stubborn empiricism: a claim about mind is expected to survive a reaction-time, an eye movement, or a surprise in the error pattern.`,
    lineage: `The lineage runs through Helmholtz's unconscious inference, through Donders' mental chronometry, and through [[james|William James]]'s long, unruly textbook, which already contained attention, habit, and the stream. Then came the drought: Watson and Skinner made the interior unprofessional. The return was a coalition. Information theory, [[turing|Alan Turing]]'s computation, and the digital computer gave psychologists a vocabulary of codes and procedures. George Miller's magical number, Broadbent's filter, and Neisser's 1967 christening of the field made a research program out of a mood. [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]]'s review of Skinner, and the rise of [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] as a window on innate structure, pulled [[language|Language]] into the same revolution. [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] was recovered as a prophet of schema; [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]] and Tversky later showed that judgment had a cognitive psychology of its own. The present field is that coalition still arguing, now with scanners, large models, and a less innocent idea of what an experiment can settle.`,
    tension: `The open problem is not a shortage of findings; it is a surplus of them, poorly bound. Cognitive psychology produces reliable effects — Stroop, priming, change blindness, loss aversion — and then disagrees about the architecture that would make those effects cohere. Box-and-arrow models look dated beside [[neural-networks|Neural Networks]]; the networks look underspecified beside a good process theory. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] asks whether the laboratory's sitting, screen-facing subject is a mind at all or only a mind in a vise. [[consciousness|Consciousness]] and [[emotion|Emotion]] were long treated as noise and have returned as data, destabilizing purely computational stories. Replication crises taught the field that clever tasks are not the same as sturdy phenomena. The atlas keeps the topic central because the questions are right even when the boxes are provisional.`,
  },
  "language": {
    lede: `[[language|Language]] is the combinatorial trick that lets a finite mind say infinitely many things, and then mean them. It is a biological capacity, a social institution, and a formal object, and the atlas keeps all three in view because none of them is a metaphor for the others. [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]] made syntax a natural science; [[wittgenstein|Ludwig Wittgenstein]] made use a philosophical problem; children, meanwhile, solve [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] before either camp has finished its argument. In this vault the topic borders [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] and [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]], because whatever language is, machines now imitate its surface with unnerving fluency.`,
    idea: `A language is not a bag of words. It is a system that pairs sound or gesture with meaning by recursive structure, and it does so under the pressure of a community that will not wait for a theory. Syntax, semantics, and pragmatics name three cuts through that system: how expressions are built, what they are about, and what a speaker is doing by producing them. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] times the cuts; [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] looks for their tissue; philosophy of [[language|Language]] asks whether meaning is in the head, in the world, or in the game. [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] is the developmental pressure test: if a theory cannot explain how a child gets from a lean diet of examples to a productive grammar, the theory is incomplete. The present moment adds a fourth pressure. Large models trained on text can continue a sentence without sharing a childhood, a body, or a stake in being believed. That fact does not settle what language is. It does force the topic to say, more carefully than before, which of its properties were secretly properties of living speakers.`,
    lineage: `Grammarians have been mapping language since Panini's sutras and Dionysius Thrax, but they mapped it as art, not as organ. Comparative philology in the nineteenth century found deep time in sound change; Saussure then split the sign and made structure the object. The cognitive turn arrived with [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]]'s Syntactic Structures in 1957, which treated grammar as a generative procedure and competence as a biological endowment. That move bound [[language|Language]] to [[rationalism|Rationalism]] and to the poverty of the stimulus. A rival lineage — usage-based, statistical, social — ran through Firth, Halliday, and later Tomasello, insisting that [[learning|Learning]] from distribution and from joint attention is enough. [[wittgenstein|Ludwig Wittgenstein]]'s later work, and the ordinary-language philosophers, kept the other border open: meaning as use, not as hidden calculus. The computational era, from early parsers to today's models, is a third lineage still being metabolized. The atlas files them as neighbors, not as a verdict.`,
    tension: `The live fight is over innateness, embodiment, and whether fluency without understanding is still language. [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]]'s universal grammar remains a research program for some and a dogma for others; the poverty-of-stimulus argument is either a theorem or a failure to count the child's data. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] claims that meaning is grounded in sensorimotor systems, which would make a disembodied model a mimic rather than a speaker. Pragmatics and [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] add that an utterance is a move in a game of status, solidarity, and deceit — a fact syntax can ignore only by shrinking the topic. Machines have made the shrinkage tempting. The atlas refuses it. Language here is the full human trick, of which well-formed strings are only the audible edge.`,
  },
  "memory": {
    lede: `[[memory|Memory]] is not a warehouse. It is a reconstruction: the past as the brain tells it now, under the pressure of the present question. [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] already knew this in 1932, watching Cambridge students recast a folk tale until it matched their own habits of thought. The topic still lives between that reconstructive insight and the stubborn fact that some traces are stable enough to swear by. [[working-memory|Working memory]] is the small, expensive present of remembering; long-term stores are its weather. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] supplies hippocampus and cortex; [[learning|Learning]] supplies the rule by which use becomes structure; [[databases|Databases]] supply the metaphor the field cannot quite quit.`,
    idea: `To remember is to do work, not to open a drawer. Encoding, consolidation, retrieval, and forgetting are verbs with costs, and each can fail in a characteristic way. [[working-memory|Working memory]] holds what thought is using; episodic memory holds what happened; semantic memory holds what is the case; procedural memory holds how to do. The divisions are useful and leaky. A skill can be told as a story; a story can freeze into a fact; a fact can vanish under stress. [[emotion|Emotion]] tags some traces for priority; [[attention|Attention]] decides what gets a chance to become a trace at all. The reconstructive view does not mean that anything goes. It means that recall is inference from remnants, constrained by schemas that [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] named and that [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] still tries to formalize. False memories, eyewitness error, and the success of spaced practice are not different subjects. They are the same machinery seen from injury, from law, and from the seminar room. A science of memory is therefore also a science of how the past is edited so that a creature can act.`,
    lineage: `Ancient ars memoriae treated remembering as architecture: palaces, loci, images placed on purpose. Ebbinghaus, in the 1880s, stripped the images away and measured nonsense syllables until forgetting became a curve. That austerity founded an experimental tradition and missed what [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] restored: that meaningful material is assimilated to prior structure. The cognitive era split stores. Atkinson and Shiffrin offered a flow from sensory to short-term to long-term; Baddeley and Hitch replaced the short-term box with [[working-memory|Working Memory]] as a workspace. Tulving distinguished episodic from semantic; Squire and others mapped the distinction onto brain systems after patient H.M. lost the ability to make new episodes. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] of consolidation, reconsolidation, and sleep then showed that a trace is not filed once. The present field inherits all of these cuts, plus a computational neighbor in [[machine-learning|Machine Learning]], which rediscovered that distributed representations forget, interfere, and generalize — sometimes on purpose.`,
    tension: `The standing difficulty is that memory is both a record and a tool, and the two jobs conflict. A perfect record would swamp action; a perfect tool would invent the past it needs. Legal systems want the record; organisms want the tool; [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] is asked to serve both and cannot. Another fault line runs between storehouse metaphors and procedural ones. [[databases|Databases]] remember by address; brains appear to remember by pattern, which is why injury and cueing behave as they do. Whether [[consciousness|Consciousness]] is required for a state to count as remembering, or whether implicit traces are memory enough, remains a boundary dispute with [[philosophy|Philosophy]]. The atlas leaves the dispute open. What it will not leave open is the warehouse: that picture is too simple to survive contact with [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] or with a single night of sleep.`,
  },
  "decision-making": {
    lede: `[[decision-making|Decision Making]] is the study of how agents choose under uncertainty, and of the shortcuts that both save and betray them. It sits at a seam between [[mind|Mind]], [[game-theory|Game Theory]], and [[ethics|Ethics]]: a choice can be described as a computation, a wager, or a responsibility. [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]] and Amos Tversky showed that the wagers humans actually take violate the axioms that a rational agent was supposed to obey. [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] then offered a cartoon of why: a fast mind that leaps, a slow mind that sometimes checks. [[probability|Probability]] supplies the mathematics of the leap; [[game-theory|Game Theory]] supplies the other player; the topic supplies the errors.`,
    idea: `A decision is a commitment made before the world has finished arriving. Classical expected-utility theory treated that commitment as a multiplication of value by chance; the cognitive science of choice treats it as a process with stages, biases, and a body. Prospect theory replaced the utility curve with a value function kinked at a reference point, and replaced raw probabilities with decision weights that overweight the rare. From there the catalog of effects — framing, anchoring, availability, loss aversion — became a map of the fast system. [[emotion|Emotion]] is not an interruption of this map; it is often the signal of value itself, as somatic-marker hypotheses and the neuropsychology of damage to affective circuitry both suggest. [[attention|Attention]] and [[working-memory|Working Memory]] limit how many options can be held in view, which is why too much information can degrade choice. When other agents are in the problem, [[game-theory|Game Theory]] and [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] take over: the rational move depends on what the other thinks you think. The field's claim on the atlas is that choosing is a mental act with a measurable anatomy, not a primitive of reason.`,
    lineage: `Pascal and Fermat made chance a calculus; Bernoulli made utility a logarithm; von Neumann and Morgenstern axiomatized the rational bet and, with [[game-theory|Game Theory]], the rational conflict. Savage and others extended the story to subjective [[probability|Probability]]. The revolt was empirical. Allais and Ellsberg showed that people violate the axioms in patterned ways; Simon named bounded rationality; [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]] and Tversky built a positive theory of the violations. Heuristics-and-biases became a research culture, then a popular book, then a policy tool in the nudge. A parallel lineage in [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] — reward prediction error, dopamine, the computation of value in prefrontal and striatal circuits — offered a biological implementation. Gigerenzer's ecological rationality, and later work on drift-diffusion models, pushed back against the idea that every departure from the axiom is a defect. The topic now lives in that argument: between a mind that fails a theorem and a mind that succeeds in an environment the theorem did not describe.`,
    tension: `The open quarrel is whether bias is a bug, a feature, or a misdescription. If humans are defective economists, then education, incentive, and [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]]'s slow system are the remedies. If humans are adapted to a world of scarce time and noisy cues, then the laboratory is the defect. A second tension is moral. [[ethics|Ethics]] and [[utilitarianism|Utilitarianism]] need a chooser who can own an act; the psychology of framing suggests the owner is partly a construction of the question. [[free-will|Free Will]] sits on this fault. A third tension is political: once choice architecture is a technology, [[democracy|Democracy]] must decide who may design the architecture. The atlas records the effects because they are real. It records the quarrel because a list of biases is not yet a theory of a life.`,
  },
  "perception": {
    lede: `[[perception|Perception]] is the controlled hallucination by which a brain meets a world it cannot touch raw. Light, pressure, and chemical gradients arrive as incomplete, noisy, delayed signals; what we live inside is an inference that has been running since before we had a word for inference. The [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]] makes that claim mathematical: perception as prediction, prediction error as news. [[attention|Attention]] decides which news may enter; [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] insists that the body is not a camera on a stick. [[empiricism|Empiricism]] once thought the senses were a window. This topic is the science of why the window is a model.`,
    idea: `To perceive is to solve an inverse problem under time pressure. The retinal image is two-dimensional and ambiguous; the world is not. Helmholtz called the solution unconscious inference; Gregory called it a hypothesis; Gibson denied the need for hypothesis and pointed to invariants in the optic array. The field still lives in that argument, now with better tools. [[fourier-analysis|Fourier Analysis]] revealed that early vision is a decomposition into spatial frequencies; [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] found cells tuned to those frequencies, to edges, to faces, to motion. Predictive-processing accounts recast the hierarchy as a cascade of guesses, each level trying to explain the one below. Illusions, then, are not party tricks. They are the seams where the guess shows. [[attention|Attention]] is the allocation of scarce computing to some seams rather than others; without it, change blindness and inattentional blindness remind us that a world can be in the light and still unmade. [[phenomenology|Phenomenology]] and [[qualia|Qualia]] ask what the made world feels like from inside, a question the computational story can postpone but not delete.`,
    lineage: `Alhazen dissected the camera of the eye; Descartes and Locke made sensation the raw material of knowing; Kant replied that the raw is already formatted. The experimental lineage is nineteenth-century: Weber, Fechner, Helmholtz, and the first psychophysics, which dared to put numbers on a just-noticeable difference. The Gestalt psychologists then showed that organization — grouping, figure, ground — is not a late luxury but an early fact. The cognitive era added information-processing diagrams and, with Marr, a three-level plea: computation, algorithm, implementation. Gibson's ecological psychology, and later [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]], refused the diagrams' hidden premise of a passive snapshot. The Bayesian turn, from signal detection through Helmholtz reborn in computational neuroscience, is the latest attempt to unify cue combination, prior knowledge, and the feeling of seeing. [[james|William James]] had already said that the world is a booming confusion until [[attention|Attention]] carves it. The topic is still carving.`,
    tension: `The fault is whether perception delivers the world or a useful fiction. Direct realists and Gibsonians claim that, under ordinary movement, the optic array specifies what is there; constructivists and Bayesians claim that specification is always a bet. Both can point to data. A second fault is the hard leftover: even a perfect inference engine does not explain why seeing feels like something, which is why [[consciousness|Consciousness]] and [[qualia|Qualia]] keep leaking into perceptual science. A third is engineering. Machine vision now classifies with superhuman speed and still fails in ways no animal would, which suggests that [[perception|Perception]] is not classification. The atlas keeps the topic next to [[empiricism|Empiricism]] as a warning: the senses are evidence, and evidence is never uninterpreted.`,
  },
  "social-psychology": {
    lede: `[[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] is the science of minds in company — conformity, persuasion, identity, cruelty, and the strange intelligence of a crowd. A person alone is already a hypothesis; a person watched, ranked, or recruited becomes a different experiment. The topic sits between [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive Psychology]] and [[political-philosophy|Political Philosophy]], because the same creature who fails a reasoning task also votes, obeys, and loves a flag. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] asks when the group is wiser than the member; [[emotion|Emotion]] asks how feeling jumps the gap between bodies. [[democracy|Democracy]] is one of the field's unplanned laboratories.`,
    idea: `Social psychology treats the situation as a causal force, often stronger than the traits we advertise as character. Asch's line judgments, Milgram's shocks, and the Stanford prison study — however contested in replication and ethics — installed the lesson that roles, norms, and authority can commandeer behavior. Later work became more cognitive: attribution, schemas, implicit bias, the construction of self in the mirror of others. [[emotion|Emotion]] is social in origin as well as in display; embarrassment, pride, and moral outrage require an audience, even an imagined one. [[decision-making|Decision Making]] in groups adds cascade, polarization, and the quiet death of dissent. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] and [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] show the other face: under the right architecture, many minds can correct a single mind's error. The field's methods — experiments, field studies, now digital traces — keep revealing that the unit of analysis is not always the individual. Sometimes it is the pair, the team, the institution, or the feed. The atlas files the topic in [[mind|Mind]] because the social is not an afterthought to cognition. It is one of cognition's native environments.`,
    lineage: `Crowd psychology of the late nineteenth century — Le Bon, Tarde — treated the group as a descent. American social psychology after the First World War, and then after the Second, treated it as a problem of democracy: how could ordinary people do extraordinary harm, and how could they be inoculated. Lewin's field theory, Sherif's autokinetic norm, and the postwar boom in experimental social psychology made the laboratory a miniature of the street. The cognitive revolution then rewired the field: the person became a naive scientist, then a cognitive miser, then a motivated reasoner. Festinger's dissonance, Tajfel's minimal groups, and Kahneman-adjacent work on social judgment bound the topic to [[decision-making|Decision Making]]. The replication crisis hit this field harder than most, forcing a moral and methodological reckoning. What survives is not a museum of famous studies but a narrower, more honest claim: situations are powerful, identities are quick to form, and [[networks|Networks]] of influence are now measurable at the scale of [[internet|The Internet]].`,
    tension: `The standing danger is overclaim. A clever laboratory situation is not a theory of history, and a failed replication is not a proof that people are unmoved by peers. The field must live between those errors. Another tension is political. Findings about persuasion, identity, and obedience are dual-use; they interest both civic educators and propagandists. [[ethics|Ethics]] here is not an appendix. A third tension is the unit of mind. If [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] is right that the self is partly a situation, then [[consciousness|Consciousness]] and [[free-will|Free Will]] need social conditions, not only neurons. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] offers a hopeful reading; groupthink and polarization offer the other. The atlas keeps both, because a science of company that only flattered the company would not be a science.`,
  },
  "chomsky": {
    lede: `[[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]] rebuilt linguistics as a branch of [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive Psychology]] and, in the same gesture, as a chapter of [[biology|Biology]]. Born in 1928, he argued that [[language|Language]] is not a cultured habit of words but a biological organ with a deep, shared grammar. Syntactic Structures (1957) and the later Aspects made competence — the tacit knowledge of a speaker — the proper object, and performance a noisy shadow. The poverty of the stimulus, the centerpiece of his case for innateness, still organizes [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]]. He is filed here as a figure because the field still argues in his vocabulary, even when it argues against him.`,
    idea: `The work is a bet that the interesting facts about language are facts about a mind, not about a corpus. A generative grammar is a finite procedure that enumerates an infinite set of well-formed sentences; explanatory adequacy requires that the procedure be learnable by a child under ordinary exposure. From this demand came universal grammar, the principles-and-parameters picture, and later the Minimalist Program, which tried to reduce the innate residue to the thinnest possible computational operation. Merge, in that late style, is the name for putting two objects together and calling the result an object — a severity of apparatus that pleased some theorists and starved others. He attacked behaviorist [[learning|Learning]], then statistical learners, then the claim that next-token models capture competence. The attacks are of a piece: they defend a distinction between a system that knows a language and a device that approximates its outputs. Whether that distinction survives contact with current [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] is now one of the atlas's live edges. His political writing, however continuous in his own life, is filed elsewhere; here the node is the linguist who made [[rationalism|Rationalism]] experimental.`,
    lineage: `The biography matters because the century does. A child of Hebrew teachers in Philadelphia, Chomsky studied at Penn with Zellig Harris, then at Harvard and MIT, where the new computers and the new cognitive mood were already in the air. Cartesian Linguistics (1966) claimed ancestors in Port-Royal and in Descartes, placing the project inside [[rationalism|Rationalism]] rather than inside [[empiricism|Empiricism]]. The 1959 review of Skinner's Verbal Behavior is a historical weapon: it helped end a research program and still gets assigned as a founding document of the cognitive revolution. Decades of intra-linguistic war followed — generative semantics, connectionism, optimality theory, usage-based grammar — each defining itself against a Chomsky. Meanwhile [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] laboratories tested poverty-of-stimulus claims with children, creoles, and home-sign, with mixed comfort for the innateness thesis. The later political Chomsky, the critic of media and empire, is the same temperament applied to another grammar: the hidden rules of public speech. The atlas keeps the linguist in [[mind|Mind]] and lets the rest of the graph pull.`,
    tension: `The afterlife is a split. In some departments Chomsky is the last great rationalist of language; in others he is the reason linguistics spent fifty years ignoring data. Statistical learners and neural models now acquire syntactic regularities from far more data than a child sees, which either refutes poverty of stimulus or changes the subject by changing the diet. Typologists complain that universal grammar was never universal; laboratory developmentalists complain it was never specified enough to fail cleanly. Defenders reply that the object was always competence, and that a simulator of performance is not a theory of knowledge. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] and social-pragmatic accounts add that a grammar without joint attention, gesture, and a body may be a beautiful organ that no infant actually grows. The node remains load-bearing because even the refutations are still shaped like his questions.`,
  },
  "kahneman": {
    lede: `[[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]] mapped the biases that live inside reason, and in doing so changed what [[decision-making|Decision Making]] could mean. Born in 1934 in Tel Aviv, later a child of occupied France, he trained, with Amos Tversky, to treat judgment as an experimental object rather than an axiom. Prospect theory and the two-system cartoon of [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] moved from papers into a 2002 Nobel Prize and then into common speech. Thinking, Fast and Slow is the public monument; the atlas files the person because the field still runs on distinctions he named. [[probability|Probability]] was supposed to be the mind's hidden logic. He showed it was often the mind's costume.`,
    idea: `The joint work with Tversky is a catalog that became a theory. People do not multiply utilities by probabilities; they evaluate gains and losses against a reference point, feel losses more steeply, and distort the chances of the rare. They judge likelihood by ease of retrieval, similarity to a stereotype, and the first number that happens to be lying around. Those heuristics are not insults. They are the fast system's tools, usually cheap and sometimes catastrophic. [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]], in Kahneman's popularization, names the yoking of that fast system to a slower, more effortful one that can follow a rule if it notices the need. The slower system is lazy. Overconfidence, planning fallacy, and the narrative fallacy are what happen when it fails to notice. Later work on well-being distinguished the experiencing self from the remembering self, binding the project back to [[memory|Memory]] and to [[emotion|Emotion]] as data, not as noise. The through-line is empirical humility about Homo economicus. A mind is a device that reaches conclusions first and reasons afterward, and the device can be measured.`,
    lineage: `The life supplies the temperament. A Jewish childhood under Vichy, a psychology degree at the Hebrew University, and a dissatisfaction with what personality research could predict led Kahneman toward perception and attention before judgment. The collaboration with Tversky, beginning in the late 1960s, was a conversation that became a method: find a neat normative rule, then find the patterned way people break it. Israel, Oregon, British Columbia, and Princeton were the waystations; the papers on small numbers, on prospect theory, on framing, were the cargo. Economics absorbed the cargo slowly, then suddenly, as behavioral economics. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] absorbed it as a chapter on judgment. The 2011 book made the two systems a piece of public furniture, for better and worse. Tversky's death in 1996 left the Nobel to Kahneman alone, a fact he treated as an injustice of the prize's rules and of mortality. The atlas keeps both names on the edge even when only one has a node.`,
    tension: `The arguments after Kahneman are about whether the map of biases is a theory or a cabinet of curiosities. Gigerenzer and the ecological-rationality school claim that many 'errors' are adaptive in the wild and that the laboratory's norms are the oddity. Replication and effect-size debates have trimmed some findings without deleting the central picture. [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] is accused of being a metaphor that hardened into anatomy; neuroimaging has not delivered two clean systems. Nudge policies built on this work can help people save or shade into manipulation, a problem for [[ethics|Ethics]] and [[democracy|Democracy]]. Kahneman himself grew more skeptical of social-psychological theater and more interested in noise — the unpatterned scatter of judgments. The figure remains because the questions he made ordinary — what is a bias, who sets the norm, how fast is thought — did not retire with the book tour.`,
  },
  "james": {
    lede: `[[james|William James]] is the psychologist-philosopher of the stream of [[consciousness|Consciousness]], of habit, of [[attention|Attention]], and of the will to believe. Born in 1842 into a New England family that took the soul seriously and Europe as a classroom, he wrote The Principles of Psychology (1890) as if a science could still be a literature. He made [[emotion|Emotion]] a bodily event, thought a stream rather than a chain of ideas, and [[attention|Attention]] the act by which a world is cut from chaos. Pragmatism was his later public philosophy; American psychology still lives in his metaphors. [[phenomenology|Phenomenology]] recognized a cousin, sometimes an ancestor.`,
    idea: `James's psychology is a defense of lived experience against both the atomism of British association and the new laboratory's temptation to kill what it measures. The stream of consciousness is the central image: thought is continuous, personal, changing, and selective. There are no discrete ideas like beads; there are substantive parts and transitive parts, the flights and perchings of a bird. [[attention|Attention]] is how selection happens, and it is effortful — a fact that later capacity theories would quantify and that James already treated as moral. The James–Lange view of [[emotion|Emotion]] reversed the folk order: we do not run because we fear; we fear because we find ourselves running. The body is not a servant of the mind; it is one of the mind's ways of happening. Habit, in his pages, is the flywheel of society and of character, a conservative power that [[learning|Learning]] both requires and risks. The will, including the will to believe, is the name for attending to one hypothesis until it becomes a world. Later [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] and William James studies would claim him; he is larger than the claims.`,
    lineage: `The household was a curriculum: Henry James Sr.'s Swedenborgian intensity, a brother who became a novelist of consciousness, sister Alice's fierce interior, and years of illness and indecision. Training in painting, then in chemistry and medicine at Harvard, then in physiology in Germany, gave James more sciences than he would ever pick one of. He taught anatomy, psychology, and philosophy at Harvard, helping to found the first American psychology course while never quite becoming a laboratory man in Wundt's sense. The Principles is the monument of 1890; The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902) took the same method to conversion, saintliness, and the sick soul. Pragmatism and radical empiricism were the late metaphysics: truth as what works in the way of thinking, experience as a world of relations, not of substances. He died in 1910, already a transatlantic figure. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] would later quote him on attention and ignore him on the soul; [[phenomenology|Phenomenology]] would find in the stream a method. Both inheritances are real.`,
    tension: `The afterlife is a struggle over which James counts. Experimentalists want the attention chapter and the habit chapter, trimmed of metaphysics. Philosophers want the pragmatist and the radical empiricist, trimmed of the psychology. Religious readers want Varieties, trimmed of the medical materialism he both used and mocked. The James–Lange theory of [[emotion|Emotion]] was revised, rejected, revived, and is now a historical name for a live idea: that interoception is not a footnote to feeling. His defense of the will to believe still scandalizes anyone who wants [[epistemology|Epistemology]] kept clean of hope. The atlas refuses the trims. James is useful because he would not specialize past the point where [[consciousness|Consciousness]] is still someone's. That refusal is either the origin of a science or a warning that the science will always leak. The node holds both readings.`,
  },
  "bartlett": {
    lede: `[[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]] showed that remembering is reconstructing, not replaying, and in doing so gave [[memory|Memory]] its modern plot. Born in 1886, a Cambridge psychologist who ran a laboratory through two wars, he asked people to recall a Native American story, 'The War of the Ghosts,' and watched the story become English. Omissions, rationalizations, and imported clichés were not noise. They were the mind fitting the strange to a schema. Remembering (1932) is the book; the idea is older than the cognitive revolution that later claimed it. [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] still needs a word for those schemas. He supplied the wound that made the word necessary.`,
    idea: `Bartlett's claim is that an act of recall is an act of construction guided by attitude, social convention, and prior structure. A schema is not a static file. It is an organized setting that both enables and distorts, the way a genre enables a story and forbids another. Serial reproduction — passing a memory along a chain of rememberers — made the distortions cumulative and visible, an experimental cousin of rumor and of folklore. Repeated reproduction by one person showed that the same drift happens inside a single mind over time. The moral for [[memory|Memory]] research is that meaning is not an extra layer on a trace; meaning is the medium in which traces live. Ebbinghaus had measured forgetting by emptying the material of meaning; Bartlett measured remembering by restoring it. Both were right about their objects. The atlas keeps Bartlett because the objects that matter for life — testimony, history, identity — are his, not the nonsense syllables. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] later split schema into scripts, frames, and models; the debt is acknowledged more often than it is paid.`,
    lineage: `The Cambridge context is the method. Bartlett studied with Ward and Rivers, absorbed anthropology as well as psychophysics, and treated the laboratory as a place where culture could be watched in miniature. He was not a behaviorist and not yet a cognitivist; he was a Cambridge man of the unconventional sort, interested in skill, fatigue, and the social. Work on war neurosis and on applied psychology during the First World War, and later the directorship of the Cambridge Psychological Laboratory, kept the questions practical. Remembering arrived in 1932, too early for the information-processing fashion and too literate for a field about to fall in love with the computer. It was rediscovered when schemas became respectable again, in the 1960s and 1970s, as [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] and reading research needed structures that could assimilate the new to the known. Head's neurological schemas, Piaget's developmental ones, and Bartlett's social-cognitive ones are cousins, not copies. He died in 1969, knighted, having made Cambridge a world center without ever reducing memory to a store.`,
    tension: `The argument after Bartlett is how far construction may go before the word memory becomes a courtesy. If every recall is a story, what is a true testimony. Courts, historians, and therapists cannot treat that as a seminar puzzle. Experimentalists sometimes accused him of looseness: too few controls, too much interpretation, a literary method pretending to be a result. The accusation has a point and misses the object. His object was meaningful recall, which resists the controls that made Ebbinghaus clean. A later tension is ownership. Schema theory in [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] can sound like Bartlett without the social, a computational skeleton without the Cambridge flesh. False-memory research confirmed the dangers he implied. The node stays because any serious account of [[memory|Memory]] still has to say whether it is answering Bartlett or only postponing him.`,
  },
  "working-memory": {
    lede: `[[working-memory|Working Memory]] is the small, expensive workspace in which thought is actually done. It is not a warehouse of the past but a present tense of [[memory|Memory]]: a handful of items, kept active, protected from interference, and lost if [[attention|Attention]] looks away. Miller's seven plus or minus two was the folklore; Baddeley and Hitch's 1974 model — phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, central executive — was the architecture that replaced the folklore. Capacity limits here explain why mental arithmetic has a shape, why a phone number dies in noise, and why [[consciousness|Consciousness]] feels narrow. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] treats the limit as a fact of mind; [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] looks for its circuit.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that online cognition needs a buffer that is neither a sense-impression nor a long-term store. Items in working memory are maintained by rehearsal and by attention, coded in mixed formats, and bound into chunks whose size depends on prior [[learning|Learning]]. The central executive is not a homunculus with opinions; it is the name for control — switching, inhibiting, updating — the same family of functions that [[attention|Attention]] research studies under other headings. Later revisions added an episodic buffer to bind across codes, acknowledging that a remembered scene is not just a sound plus a picture. Individual differences in this capacity predict reasoning, reading, and fluid intelligence more stubbornly than most cognitive measures, which is why the concept leaked into education and into debates about [[decision-making|Decision Making]] under load. Load is the key experimental verb: add a second task, lengthen the list, interrupt rehearsal, and watch a process that looked purely logical reveal its workspace. [[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] often treats working memory as the slow system's fuel. When the fuel is short, the fast system drives.`,
    lineage: `The ancestry is short-term memory: James's primary memory, Broadbent's filter, Atkinson and Shiffrin's box. The box was too passive. People did not merely store a few items; they manipulated them, and two tasks could interfere or not depending on code. Baddeley and Hitch split the box into slave systems and a control process, a move that saved the phenomena and created twenty years of dual-task industry. Cowan later offered an embedded-processes view: working memory as the activated subset of long-term [[memory|Memory]], with a focus of [[attention|Attention]] of about four items. Oberauer, Engle, and others made control, not storage, the scarce resource. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] found persistent activity in prefrontal and parietal circuits, then found that activity is more dynamic than a simple holding pattern. The word remains, even as the box diagrams yellow, because every theory of thought still needs a name for the tiny stage on which the present is arranged.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is say, without remainder, what is being stored. Is working memory a place, a state of activation, a skill of attention, or a set of pointers into long-term structure. Each answer fits some data and spoils others. The magical numbers keep shrinking under better methods, which suggests that the folklore of seven was a measurement of chunking, not of slots. Educational translations — train the workspace, raise the intelligence — have mostly underperformed, a humbling for anyone who wanted a gymnasium of the mind. There is also a phenomenological leftover: the workspace is often identified with [[consciousness|Consciousness]], but subliminal maintenance and unattended traces complicate the identification. The atlas keeps the node because the limit is real even when the model furniture is not. Thought happens in a small room.`,
  },
  "dual-process": {
    lede: `[[dual-process|Dual Process Theory]] is the picture of a fast, associative mind yoked to a slow, deliberative one. The cartoon is famous: System 1 leaps, System 2 checks, and most of life is leaps. [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]] gave the cartoon its public face; Stanovich, Evans, and others gave it a research program in reasoning and [[decision-making|Decision Making]]. The theory tries to explain why a creature that can do logic so often will not, and why [[working-memory|Working Memory]] load makes the will-not worse. It is filed as a concept because it is a working partition of [[mind|Mind]], not a pair of organs found in a dissection.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that two families of process compete and cooperate in judgment. Type 1 processes are rapid, parallel, automatic, and opaque to introspection; they deliver intuitions shaped by [[learning|Learning]], [[emotion|Emotion]], and associative structure. Type 2 processes are slow, sequential, capacity-limited, and capable of following a rule that contradicts the intuition. The rule-following costs [[attention|Attention]] and [[working-memory|Working Memory]]; hence the laziness of System 2 and the need for cues that an error is likely. In reasoning research, belief bias and the conjunction fallacy become signs that the fast output was accepted. In moral psychology, a parallel split has been proposed between gut condemnation and utilitarian calculation, tying the concept to [[ethics|Ethics]]. The partition is a bet about architecture, not a claim that a person has two selves. Hybrid models now speak of default-intervention rather than of two homunculi: the fast answer is the default, and the slow system sometimes intervenes. That revision keeps the phenomena while softening the metaphysics. The atlas records both the cartoon and the repair.`,
    lineage: `The split is old. Plato's charioteer, Aristotle's rational and irrational parts, and the Enlightenment's reason-versus-passion already knew a yoking. The modern experimental lineage runs through Schneider and Shiffrin's automatic versus controlled processing, through Posner's work on [[attention|Attention]], and through Epstein's experiential versus rational systems. Evans distinguished heuristic and analytic processes in reasoning; Stanovich bound the distinction to individual differences and to the failure of intelligence tests to predict bias. [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]]'s synthesis made the vocabulary public and, by making it public, made it crude. Critics from single-process and unimodel camps argued that one continuum of effort could do the work, and that two systems is a story the field tells itself. Neuroimaging was recruited by both sides and settled neither. The concept survives because teachers, clinicians, and researchers still need a way to say that not all thought is the same kind of work. Survival is not the same as proof.`,
    tension: `What the theory cannot yet do is point to two systems with independent failure modes, as a cardiologist points to two chambers. The labels multiply — hot and cold, associative and rule-based, implicit and explicit — and it is not clear they cut at the same joint. [[free-will|Free Will]] debates sometimes hijack the slow system as the seat of agency, which is more theology than data. Social psychologists have used the split to explain prejudice as automatic and control as virtuous, a moralization that [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] itself has had to qualify. Once a reader learns the cartoon, every error looks like System 1 and every success like System 2, a just-so story. The atlas keeps the concept because the phenomena of speed, effort, and intervention are real. It keeps the tension because a cartoon that explains everything has started to explain nothing.`,
  },
  "embodied-cognition": {
    lede: `[[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] is the claim that thinking is not a disembodied program but a skill of a body in a world. Hands, posture, gait, and the layout of a room are not peripherals to a central code; they are part of the machinery that makes a thought possible. The concept is a revolt against the mid-century picture of mind as software, a picture borrowed from [[computing|Computing]] and then mistaken for a discovery. [[perception|Perception]] becomes an action, not a snapshot; [[language|Language]] borrows spatial and motor structure; [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] is asked why a robot that cannot fall over should be said to know. [[phenomenology|Phenomenology]] had been waiting for this laboratory.`,
    idea: `The working idea comes in strengths. A modest version says that the body constrains and scaffolds cognition: gesture helps counting, posture leaks into mood, a tool extends the reaching mind. A stronger version says that the vehicles of thought are themselves sensorimotor, so that an amodal symbol system is a philosopher's fiction. The strongest version, sometimes called enaction, says that a mind is the history of a system's structural coupling with a niche — a claim that meets [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] and the biology of [[maturana|Humberto Maturana]] on shared ground. [[perception|Perception]], on Gibsonian and enactive readings, is not the painting of an inner model but the pickup of affordances for action. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] of canonical neurons, interoception, and predictive motor control can be recruited by these views without settling them. The concept also rewrites [[emotion|Emotion]]: feeling is not a colored overlay on a cold judgment but a bodily appraisal that is the judgment. Critics dare the view to predict as well as the old computational theory. The dare is fair. The atlas keeps the concept because that theory's successes were real and its brain-in-a-vat picture was never an experiment, only a habit.`,
    lineage: `Merleau-Ponty, before the name existed, described the body as the vehicle of being-in-the-world; Heidegger made readiness-to-hand more basic than representation. [[james|William James]] had already put the body inside [[emotion|Emotion]] and habit. The cognitive-science naming came later: Varela, Thompson, and Rosch's The Embodied Mind (1991); Lakoff and Johnson's metaphors that live in flesh; Clark's extended mind; Barsalou's grounded cognition; Brooks's robots that did without inner maps. [[maturana|Humberto Maturana]] and Varela's autopoietic biology supplied a lineage that never granted the software metaphor in the first place. Gibson's ecological psychology, long a minority report inside [[perception|Perception]], became newly respectable as a precursor. The concept is thus a coalition of phenomenology, robotics, linguistics, and biology, united less by a theorem than by an enemy: the idea that a mind is a theorem-prover with sensors attached. That enemy was productive. Embodied cognition is the name for what had to be said after the productivity showed its cost.`,
    tension: `What the idea cannot yet do is specify, for a given task, which parts of the body are constitutive of the thought and which are mere scaffolding. If a notebook can be part of [[memory|Memory]], the concept risks a boundless inflation in which everything coupled counts as mind. If only the brain's simulation of the body counts, the revolt collapses back into internalism with extra imagery. Experimental programs — action-sentence compatibility, facial-feedback, grounded concepts — have mixed replicability, which enemies treat as a refutation and friends as a measurement problem. [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] remains a pressure test: a disembodied model that talks fluently either falsifies the strong claim or shows that talk was never the right test. The atlas leaves the strength of the claim open. What it will not leave open is the innocence of the software picture. That innocence is gone.`,
  },
  "attention": {
    lede: `[[attention|Attention]] is the spotlight, the bottleneck, the scarce resource that makes a world out of too much signal. [[james|William James]] wrote that everyone knows what attention is, which was a joke at the expense of a science that still does not. Selection is the common verb: some objects, features, and thoughts are enhanced, others dropped, and the dropping is as important as the light. Without attention, [[perception|Perception]] is a crowd, [[working-memory|Working Memory]] is a leak, and [[consciousness|Consciousness]] has nothing to be of. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] times the bottleneck; [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] hunts its gain control; the concept remains the name for a limit we live inside.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that an organism cannot, and should not, process everything that strikes its sensors. Early-selection theories put the filter near sensation; late-selection theories put it near action; load theories make the locus depend on how hard the primary task is. Feature integration, endogenous versus exogenous control, and the distinction between alerting, orienting, and executive networks are later carvings of the same scarce stuff. Attention is not one mechanism. It is a family of mechanisms that share a function: prioritizing. Salience maps in vision, cocktail-party selection in hearing, and the inner spotlight that can be directed at a memory or a plan are cousins. [[emotion|Emotion]] and value bias the priority; so do instructions, habits, and other minds. Inattentional blindness and change blindness are the experimental proofs that a world can be lit and still unmade. The concept therefore binds [[perception|Perception]] to [[working-memory|Working Memory]] and to action: what is selected is what can be used. A theory of mind that treats attention as an optional extra has not yet met a crowded room.`,
    lineage: `Helmholtz already knew that attention could be shifted without the eyes. James made it a chapter and a moral fact: the education of attention as the education of the will. The information-age lineage begins with Cherry's shadowing, Broadbent's filter, Treisman's attenuation, and Deutsch and Deutsch's late selection — a twenty-year argument about where the bottleneck sits. Posner gave the field spatial cues and a clinical map of networks; Treisman gave it illusory conjunctions; Kahneman, before judgment, wrote a book on attention and effort. The imaging era found frontoparietal control systems and, in the default mode, a reminder that the mind wanders when the spotlight is unemployed. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] of neglect, ADHD, and meditation then made attention a clinical and contemplative object as well as a laboratory one. Each era kept James's joke alive: the word is obvious until one has to say what it names.`,
    tension: `The concept cannot yet say whether attention is a cause, a consequence, or a redescription of enhanced processing. If attending is just what we call the winner of a competition among representations, the spotlight is a metaphor that has been taking a salary. If it is a separate control system, one must explain who controls the controller without inviting a homunculus. [[consciousness|Consciousness]] complicates the payroll: some theories identify awareness with attention, others show attention without awareness and awareness without attention, a pairing that should be impossible if the words were synonyms. Productivity culture and the attention economy of [[internet|The Internet]] have seized the word and thinned it. The atlas keeps the laboratory meaning close to the moral one James heard, because the scarcity is both a circuit and a life. What we attend to is the world we get.`,
  },
  "learning": {
    lede: `[[learning|Learning]] is enduring change in what an organism can do, from a reflex rerouted to a proof retained. It is the verb that turns [[empiricism|Empiricism]] into structure, and it is slower, stranger, and more various than a single law. Habituation, classical conditioning, operant shaping, statistical extraction, insight, and the long climb of expertise are not one process with different costumes. [[memory|Memory]] is what learning looks like when it can be retrieved; [[machine-learning|Machine Learning]] is what it looks like when the organism is a model. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] wants synapses; education wants curricula; the concept has to survive both demands.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that a system is plastic in a way that is not mere damage and not mere growth. Plasticity is guided by error, by reward, by repetition, and by the social world that supplies problems worth the cost. Hebb's conjecture — cells that fire together wire together — is a slogan for one family of change; prediction-error accounts in the [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]] tradition are a slogan for another. Neither slogan covers a child acquiring syntax, a pianist acquiring a piece, or an immune system acquiring a memory of an insult, though all three are learning in an extended sense. [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] is the stress test: few examples, little correction, a productive system. [[working-memory|Working Memory]] and [[attention|Attention]] gate what may be learned in a sitting; sleep and consolidation decide what remains. Transfer — whether practice at one task helps another — is the unglamorous question that separates a theory of learning from a theory of performance. The atlas files the concept in [[mind|Mind]] because the change that matters here is a change in an agent, not only in a weight matrix. The neighbor in [[computing|Computing]] is real and is not a substitute.`,
    lineage: `Association is the old name: Aristotle's contiguity, Hume's habit, Thorndike's law of effect. Pavlov and Skinner made the laboratory versions, powerful and then imperial, until they tried to own [[language|Language]] and broke. Tolman's cognitive maps, Köhler's insight, and Bartlett's schemas kept a more structured learner alive through the behaviorist years. The cognitive era added strategies, metacognition, and the spacing and testing effects that every student is still asked to ignore. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] of long-term potentiation, dopamine, and hippocampal replay gave the slogans tissue. Meanwhile [[machine-learning|Machine Learning]] independently rediscovered gradient descent, overfit, and generalization, then returned the metaphors to psychology with interest. The two lineages now talk constantly, sometimes to their joint confusion. A loss function is not a childhood. The concept in this vault is the childhood, the practice, and the scar.`,
    tension: `What learning cannot yet explain with one apparatus is why some changes need a thousand trials and some need one fright. One-shot learning, catastrophic interference, and the poverty of the stimulus in [[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] all embarrass the slow-association picture without replacing it. Education policy keeps wanting a general gymnasium of the mind; the transfer data keep refusing. There is also a political tension. If [[learning|Learning]] is mostly the exploitation of priors, then equity of instruction is not equity of outcome, a fact that [[ethics|Ethics]] must handle without pretending the data are kinder than they are. Machines that learn from the whole internet raise a different embarrassment: they show that scale can mimic understanding, which either cheapens the word or forces a sharper definition. The atlas keeps the human verb: lasting change in an agent that can be wrong.`,
  },
  "bayesian-brain": {
    lede: `The [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]] is the thesis that the nervous system is an engine of probabilistic prediction, always updating. Priors meet likelihoods; posterior beliefs become the next priors; [[perception|Perception]] is the guess that currently best explains the sensory rain. Helmholtz's unconscious inference is the ancestor; [[probability|Probability]] is the language; [[machine-learning|Machine Learning]] is the neighboring workshop. [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] uses the thesis to unify cue combination, motor control, and some illusions under one arithmetic. It is a concept, not yet a complete anatomy, and it has the ambitions of a complete anatomy.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that the brain cannot wait for certainty and so computes with distributions. A percept is a posterior; an action is a sample or a control policy that minimizes expected surprise; [[attention|Attention]] is precision, the weighting of some prediction errors over others. In predictive-processing versions associated with Friston, the whole organism is in the business of minimizing free energy, a claim that reaches from [[perception|Perception]] toward [[emotion|Emotion]], interoception, and even [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]]. More modest versions stay close to psychophysics: given two noisy cues, humans often combine them near the Bayesian optimum. The modest versions are well supported. The imperial versions are a research program. Either way, the concept recasts error as news rather than as failure, which is why it talks so easily to [[learning|Learning]]. A prior is just [[memory|Memory]] with a probability attached. The atlas files the idea here because it is a theory of mind first, and a borrowing from statistics second. Invert that order and one has a metaphor. Keep it and one has a bet that can fail in a laboratory.`,
    lineage: `Bayes's theorem is eighteenth-century; Helmholtz made it a psychology without the notation. Signal detection theory, then Marr, then the 1990s work of Knill, Richards, and others on vision as Bayesian inference, made the notation official. Machine learning's graphical models and later deep generative models supplied both algorithms and a culture of thinking in latents. Friston's papers, dense and polarizing, tried to make a single principle do the work of many, recruiting [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] of hierarchy and of neuromodulation as precision control. Clark, Hohwy, and the philosophers of mind then translated the program for [[consciousness|Consciousness]] studies, with mixed welcome. The lineage is therefore a braid of psychophysics, computational [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]], and [[philosophy|Philosophy]], each suspicious of the others' standards of proof. That suspicion is healthy. A theorem is not a brain, and a brain that can be described as Bayesian on Tuesdays may be something else on Wednesday's task.`,
    tension: `What the thesis cannot yet do is say where the priors come from without smuggling in the knowledge it was supposed to explain. Too strong a prior and the organism hallucinates; too weak and it is blown about by noise. Development, culture, and [[evolution|Evolution]] are all nominated as sources, which is to say the problem is deferred. A model with free priors can fit opposed behaviors, which makes falsification a social achievement. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] asks whether inner inference is the right picture at all, or whether a body already coupled to a niche needs less representation than the math suggests. And [[consciousness|Consciousness]] remains unpaid: even a perfect inferential engine does not explain why the posterior feels like a world. The atlas keeps the concept because the arithmetic of uncertainty is clearly part of mind. Part is not all.`,
  },
  "language-acquisition": {
    lede: `[[language-acquisition|Language Acquisition]] is the feat by which children become native speakers from a lean diet of examples. No curriculum, little correction, a few years, and then a generative system that can scold, lie, and rhyme. [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]] named the puzzle the poverty of the stimulus and treated it as evidence for an innate grammar. Usage-based and statistical learners treat the same puzzle as evidence that the diet was never as lean as the innatists claimed. [[learning|Learning]] theory, [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]], and [[language|Language]] itself all meet in the nursery, which is why the concept is not a footnote to linguistics.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that a learner must go from finite, messy input to a productive grammar, and that the route reveals the architecture of [[mind|Mind]]. Milestones — babbling, first words, a vocabulary spurt, combinatorial speech, over-regularization — are the public timetable. Under the timetable sit debates. Is there a critical period. How much is syntax given and how much extracted. What is the role of joint attention, gesture, and the social game that [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] would recognize as a situation. Home-sign, creoles, and Nicaraguan Sign Language show that children can invent structure when the input is thin, a fact innatists love. Infant statistics — tracking syllables, mapping words to referents with surprising speed — show that the input is richer than a transcript of errors, a fact empiricists love. Both loves can be data. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] adds that a child is not a parser but a creature pointing, moving, and being moved, so that [[language|Language]] grows inside action. Computational models, from parameter-setting to neural nets, are existence proofs of possible routes, not yet a diary of any particular child.`,
    lineage: `Diary studies of the nineteenth century, including Darwin on his own son, treated the feat as natural history. Behaviorism then promised that shaping and imitation would suffice; [[chomsky|Noam Chomsky]]'s reply, and Brown's longitudinal work, made sufficiency look like a fantasy. The 1970s and 1980s built a field: learnability theory, the subset principle, the debate over negative evidence, and the first serious studies of signed languages as first languages. Connectionism in the 1980s offered past-tense models as a brief against rules; the brief did not end the rules. Tomasello and the usage-based school recentered social cognition and item-based constructions. Meanwhile [[machine-learning|Machine Learning]] showed that scale can extract syntactic regularities, reopening the poverty argument with a new kind of learner that has no childhood. The lineage is a sequence of attempts to say what the child knows that the data did not obviously contain. Each attempt redefines 'obviously.'`,
    tension: `The concept cannot yet adjudicate innateness without a shared measure of the input. If 'lean' means no negative evidence, the innatist has a point. If it means billions of statistical cues plus a social world, the empiricist has a point. Critical-period claims vary by subsystem: accent is not syntax is not vocabulary, and second-language research is a messy oracle. There is an ethical edge. Theories of delay and disorder can become destinies for children, which is why [[ethics|Ethics]] belongs in the clinic as well as in the seminar. Machines that acquire fluency without a body, without care, and without a stake in being understood either show that acquisition was misdescribed or that fluency was never the phenomenon. The atlas keeps the nursery in view. A theory of [[language|Language]] that cannot grow in a child is a theory of a museum.`,
  },
  "emotion": {
    lede: `[[emotion|Emotion]] is the evaluative color of experience that steers [[attention|Attention]], [[memory|Memory]], and [[decision-making|Decision Making]]. It is not the opposite of reason. It is often the reason's first draft: a bodily verdict on what matters, issued before a sentence is ready. [[james|William James]] reversed the folk order and made the feeling a reading of the body; later [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] found the circuits that make the reading possible. [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] adds that many emotions are public acts — displays, claims, contagions — as much as inner weather. The concept sits in [[mind|Mind]] because a creature without value would still compute, and would not care.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that an emotion is a patterned change in body, brain, and action tendency, tagged with a meaning. Fear readies escape; anger readies confrontation; sadness withdraws; disgust expels; joy goes out to the world. Whether these are basic biological modules, as Ekman argued from faces, or constructed from more general ingredients of arousal and appraisal, as Barrett argues, is a live split. Either way, the functions leak into cognition. Affective tagging decides what is encoded; mood-congruent recall decides what returns; anxiety narrows the spotlight of [[attention|Attention]]. Damasio's somatic-marker hypothesis, whatever its details, made the point that [[decision-making|Decision Making]] without feeling is not purer. It is poorer. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] takes this as obvious: a verdict that does not run through a body is not the kind of verdict animals issue. Social and moral emotions — shame, guilt, pride, awe — bind the concept to [[ethics|Ethics]] and to other minds. An emotion is therefore both a physiological event and a stance in a world of people. Theories that keep only one half keep a husk.`,
    lineage: `Darwin's The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals (1872) treated faces as lineage, not as theatre. [[james|William James]] and Lange, independently, put the body first; Cannon and Bard put the thalamus first; Schachter and Singer added a cognitive label to a stirring. Tomkins, Ekman, and Izard built the basic-emotion program; appraisal theorists from Arnold to Lazarus made meaning the hinge. [[neuroscience|Neuroscience]] of the amygdala, insula, and prefrontal regulation then gave the arguments anatomy, including the awkward finding that the same circuit participates in too many feelings to be a single 'fear center.' Constructivist and predictive-processing accounts now treat emotion as an inference about interoceptive causes, a meeting with the [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]]. Cultural psychology and history keep showing that the repertoire of named feelings is not a natural list. The lineage is therefore biology, phenomenology, and anthropology in an unstable alliance. James would have recognized the instability as the subject.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is say how many emotions there are without begging the question of what would count as one. A face, a circuit, a folk name, and an action tendency do not always coincide, which is why the basic-versus-constructed fight continues. There is a clinical tension too: if emotions are constructed, therapy is a work on concepts as much as on chemicals; if they are modules, the chemicals look more like levers. Both can be true in different cases, which is not yet a theory. A public tension follows [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]]: feeds now design feeling at scale. [[consciousness|Consciousness]] adds the unpaid bill of what a feeling feels like. The atlas keeps the concept next to choice and next to other people, because a mind that cannot be moved cannot be explained.`,
  },
  "systems": {
    lede: `[[systems|Systems]] is the continent of organized wholes — [[feedback|Feedback]], [[emergence|Emergence]], [[networks|Networks]], and the maps we make of them. It is not a department so much as a climate: a way of seeing loops where a linear story wanted a cause. [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] is the craft; [[complexity|Complexity]] is the middle kingdom between clockwork and noise; [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] is the mid-century science of steering that still haunts the vocabulary. [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] and [[cartography-of-knowledge|Cartography of Knowledge]] belong here because a map is itself a system, and this vault is one. The hub exists so that [[ecology|Ecology]], [[mind|Mind]], and [[computing|Computing]] can be read as cousins rather than as coincidences.`,
    idea: `A system is a set of relations that makes a difference: parts arranged so that the arrangement, not only the stuff, does work. The work may be regulation, as in a thermostat; growth, as in a city; meaning, as in a language; or survival, as in a cell that produces the parts that produce it. [[feedback|Feedback]] is the elementary move — outputs returning as inputs — and from that move come stability, oscillation, runaway, and [[learning|Learning]]. [[emergence|Emergence]] names the moment a level appears that the parts, inventoried separately, do not possess. [[networks|Networks]] give the relations a geometry; [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] show that the geometry is often unfair, a few hubs carrying the load. [[self-organization|Self-Organization]] and [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] explain how order can grow without a conductor; [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] asks which systems are alive by virtue of making themselves. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] and [[game-theory|Game Theory]] treat agents as parts that think, a reminder that a whole of minds is not a mindless whole. The continent's wager, which is also the [[knowledge-atlas|Knowledge Atlas]]'s wager, is that seeing the loops is a form of knowledge, not a decoration on knowledge got some other way.`,
    lineage: `Wholes are an old temptation. Aristotle's substances, Hegel's totalities, and the Gestalt cry that the whole is other than the sum, all precede the word system as a science. The twentieth century made it operational. [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] named [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] in 1948; the Macy conferences braided biologists, engineers, and anthropologists, including [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]]. Forrester's industrial dynamics, Meadows's leverage points, and the Club of Rome's models carried the craft into policy, with mixed humility. [[maturana|Humberto Maturana]] and Varela described living systems as self-producing; Prigogine and the Santa Fe Institute made [[complexity|Complexity]] a destination. [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]] and the new science of [[networks|Networks]] then gave the continent a statistical skeleton, while the [[internet|Internet]] became the skeleton's most public instance. [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] had been waiting since Euler. The hub gathers these turns because they share a refusal: the refusal to explain a pattern by a single part's virtue. That refusal is a method, and methods have histories.`,
    tension: `The standing fault is between a useful lens and a fog that makes every problem look like a loop. Systems talk can reveal leverage; it can also postpone responsibility by dissolving actors into diagrams. A second fault is scale. A cell, a firm, a biosphere, and this atlas are not the same kind of whole, and a vocabulary that pretends they are will lie about all four. [[emergence|Emergence]] is either a precise claim about levels or a mystical shrug; [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] is either a science of control or a mid-century mood. The atlas is implicated. By drawing filaments among ideas it makes connection look like explanation, which is the cartographer's original sin. Use the hub to see the loops. Then ask who drew them, who is left out, and what a part can still do that a whole cannot forgive.`,
  },
  "systems-thinking": {
    lede: `[[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] is a discipline of seeing loops, delays, and leverage rather than isolated events. A stock is not a story; a feedback is not a villain; the highest-leverage move is often far from the symptom that summoned the meeting. [[feedback|Feedback]] is the elementary literacy; [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] is the ancestral science; [[ecology|Ecology]] is the oldest classroom in which the literacy was a matter of survival. The topic is practical without being narrow: a city, a clinic, a climate, and a mind can all be misread as a pile of incidents. [[complexity|Complexity]] is what the reading finds when the loops are many and adaptive.`,
    idea: `The craft begins with a shift of unit. Instead of an event and a culprit, one looks for a structure that would produce this event again tomorrow with different names attached. Causal-loop diagrams, stock-and-flow models, and the vocabulary of reinforcing and balancing loops are the notation. Delays matter because a system with delay can be whipped into oscillation by a well-meaning hand on the tiller. Leverage matters because some nodes, when pressed, move the whole, and most nodes do not — a lesson that [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] later made topological. Donella Meadows's list of leverage points, from numbers and buffers up through rules, information flows, and the paradigm itself, is a moral ranking as much as a technical one. [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] and [[self-organization|Self-Organization]] warn the thinker not to assume a manager wherever there is order. The topic therefore sits between engineering and [[ethics|Ethics]]: to intervene in a system is to take a side in a web of delayed consequences. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] is one hoped-for outcome of a well-designed web; collapse is the other.`,
    lineage: `The craft has engineering grandparents. Watt's governor, the thermostat, and the operations research of the Second World War taught control before they taught wisdom. [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] and Ashby made the teaching a science; Forrester, at MIT, made it a method for firms and cities, then for the world model of Limits to Growth. The method was accused of arrogance and of being right too early. Both accusations still travel. Senge's learning organization brought the loops into management literature; Checkland's soft systems methodology admitted that the 'system' is often a debate, not a machine. [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]] supplied the ecological and psychiatric key: the pattern that connects, and the double bind as a systemic injury. [[ecology|Ecology]] of food webs, succession, and resilience — Holling especially — gave the field its best metaphors and some of its best data. The present lineage includes climate modeling, public health, and platform design, each a place where an event-story is a dangerous luxury.`,
    tension: `The open problem is humility. A diagram that explains everything will excuse anything, and a model that cannot be falsified is a worldview. Limits to Growth was treated as prophecy, then as failure, then as a delayed success, which is exactly how delayed systems make fools of their judges. Another tension is agency. If the structure did it, what is a responsible person. [[game-theory|Game Theory]] and political theory refuse to let the person dissolve; systems thinking sometimes seems to want the dissolving. A third tension is participation. Soft systems and critical systems traditions insist that the people inside the loops must help draw them, or the drawing is just another control. The atlas takes that insistence seriously. This vault is a system of editorial decisions. Seeing the loops includes seeing the hand that inked them.`,
  },
  "complexity": {
    lede: `[[complexity|Complexity]] names the middle kingdom between the simple and the random, where adaptive order lives. A clock is simple; a gas is random; a cell, a city, an immune system, and a language are something else — many parts, local rules, history, and a stubborn refusal to be summarized by an average. [[emergence|Emergence]] is the signature of that refusal; [[networks|Networks]] are one of its skeletons; [[entropy|Entropy]] is the budget against which the order is bought. [[chaos-theory|Chaos Theory]] showed that determinism can look like noise; complexity science asks how noise can look like design. [[evolution|Evolution]] is the oldest complexity engine the atlas knows.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that some systems are not merely complicated. Complicated systems can be taken apart and predicted if one is careful; complex ones change as they are probed, adapt to their own patterns, and grow new levels. Agents interact; interaction writes a structure; structure constrains later interaction. That loop is the weather of the topic. [[self-organization|Self-Organization]] produces pattern without a plan; [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] is one of its crafts; [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] are one of its statistics. The edge of chaos, a phrase now worn smooth, named a regime in which order is flexible enough to compute and stable enough to persist. Santa Fe-style models — fitness landscapes, artificial societies, sandpiles — are existence proofs that simple rules can grow surprising macrostates. They are not, by themselves, explanations of any particular city or cell. The topic earns its keep when it forces a question that a linear model cannot even parse: what kind of cause is a network, a delay, a cascade. [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] is the craft of living with that question; complexity is the science that tries to make the question sharp.`,
    lineage: `Nineteenth-century statistical physics taught the world to think in ensembles; Poincaré taught it that a few bodies can be enough for chaos; Turing, in a late paper on morphogenesis, taught it that chemistry can draw a leopard. von Neumann's self-reproducing automata, Wiener's [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]], and Ashby's homeostat are mid-century ancestors. Prigogine's dissipative structures made [[entropy|Entropy]] a partner of order rather than only its enemy. The Santa Fe Institute, Holland's adaptive systems, Kauffman's Boolean nets, and Wolfram's cellular automata made a visible school in the 1980s and 1990s. Meanwhile [[evolution|Evolution]], [[ecology|Ecology]], and economics had been complex all along, sometimes without wanting the brand. [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]]'s network turn, and the data of the [[internet|Internet]], gave the school new measurements. The lineage is therefore physics, biology, computation, and social science sharing a suspicion of the average as a sufficient summary.`,
    tension: `The standing danger is a brand that outruns a result. Complexity can become a compliment paid to any mess one does not yet understand. Power laws are not everywhere; agent-based models can hide as many assumptions as the equations they replaced; 'more is different' can be a license to stop reducing too soon or a warning not to reduce too far. A second tension is prediction. If the interesting systems are adaptive, then a forecast is an intervention, and the topic owes an account of its own [[feedback|Feedback]] on the world it describes. A third is political: complexity can be used to counsel patience (do not pull that lever) or to counsel daring (the system will reorganize). Both counsels can be bought. The atlas keeps the topic because the middle kingdom is real — and a kingdom is not a theory.`,
  },
  "emergence": {
    lede: `[[emergence|Emergence]] is what happens when a whole grows powers that its parts do not possess separately. Wetness is not in a molecule of water; a traffic jam is not in a driver; a mind, if it is emergent, is not in a neuron. The topic is a claim about levels, not a mood of wonder, though wonder is how the claim usually arrives. [[complexity|Complexity]] supplies the conditions; [[self-organization|Self-Organization]] supplies a mechanism; [[consciousness|Consciousness]] is the case that makes the claim urgent. [[symbiosis|Symbiosis]] and the [[origin-of-life|Origin of Life]] are older cases, written in cells rather than in arguments.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that explanation has layers, and that a complete inventory of parts can still miss a pattern that only exists at another grain. Weak emergence says the higher pattern is unexpected given our current models but is in principle deducible. Strong emergence says the pattern is not deducible even in principle, which is either a fact about nature or a failure of nerve. Most working scientists live in the weak neighborhood and use the word for phenomena that require new variables: temperature, price, genotype, belief. [[networks|Networks]] make some emergences visual: a giant component appears, a cascade runs, a hub is born by [[scale-free-networks|preferential attachment]]. [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] claims a stronger biological emergence, a unity that produces itself. The topic therefore sits on a fault between [[physics|Physics]] and the special sciences. Reduction is a research program; emergence is the name of the residue that keeps the program honest. Whether [[consciousness|Consciousness]] is residue, program, or something the vocabulary cannot yet hold is the atlas's loudest instance of the problem.`,
    lineage: `British emergentists of the early twentieth century — Alexander, Lloyd Morgan, Broad — made a philosophy of levels against both mechanism and vitalism. They lost the fashion war to a more confident reductionism, then returned as a citation whenever a science hit a level it could not cross. Anderson's 1972 'More Is Different' was the condensed-matter manifesto: symmetry breaking as a license for new laws at new scales. [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] and later Santa Fe work supplied examples from machines and models; [[biology|Biology]] supplied the rest, from termite mounds to the eukaryotic cell as a frozen [[symbiosis|Symbiosis]]. [[maturana|Humberto Maturana]] and Varela insisted that the living is an emergent organization, not a list of molecules. Philosophers of mind, facing qualia, borrowed the word and sometimes inflated it. The lineage is a series of negotiations about how much novelty a respectable science is allowed to admit. Each generation redraws the line and calls the drawing realism.`,
    tension: `The open problem is criteria. Without a test for when a new level has earned its keep, emergence is a compliment. With too strict a test, only [[physics|Physics]] is real and the rest of the atlas is bookkeeping. Downward causation — the whole constraining the parts — is either the heart of the idea or a confusion about description. [[consciousness|Consciousness]] makes the confusion expensive: if feeling strongly emerges, then physicalism has a hole; if it only weakly emerges, then the hole is in our models, and patience is the method. Simulation culture adds a temptation: because we can grow a pattern on a screen, we think we have explained the pattern in the world. Sometimes we have. The atlas keeps the topic to mark the moment a reader should ask which.`,
  },
  "networks": {
    lede: `A [[networks|Network]] is the abstract form of connection — nodes, links, and the shapes that relation can take. Brains, banks, epidemics, citations, roads, and this atlas are the same kind of object before they are different kinds of world. [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] is the mathematics; [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] are one of the empirical surprises; the [[internet|Internet]] is the public demonstration. [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]] made the surprise a science of hubs; Watts and Strogatz made small worlds a laboratory. [[knowledge-atlas|The Knowledge Atlas]] is a network that admits it, which is rarer than it sounds.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that structure of connection is a cause. Degree, clustering, path length, modularity, and centrality are not labels on a drawing; they predict how a rumor travels, how a failure cascades, and where to put a vaccine or a lie. A random graph, an ordered lattice, a small world, and a scale-free net are different climates for the same weather of contagion. [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] shows that links can be written into an environment rather than into a plan; [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] asks when the net is smarter than its nodes. [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] treats a graph as a memory; [[cartography-of-knowledge|Cartography of Knowledge]] treats it as a map. The topic therefore unifies objects that departments keep apart, which is its virtue and its risk. A social graph is not a protein interaction graph, even when both are scale-free on Tuesdays. What transfers is a set of questions: where are the hubs, what happens if they fail, who can reach whom, and which clusters are almost worlds. Those questions are how the atlas itself should be read.`,
    lineage: `Euler's Königsberg bridges made a walk into a graph. Moreno's sociograms, then the mathematical sociology of the mid-century, made people into nodes. Erdős and Rényi gave random graphs a theory; Granovetter's weak ties, Milgram's small worlds, and the six degrees of folklore gave the theory a public. The late-1990s data explosion — the web, protein maps, citation nets — let [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]], Watts, Strogatz, and Newman turn anecdotes into exponents. [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] had already supplied Eulerian paths, colorings, and flows; the new science of networks supplied growth rules, robustness, and a taste for log-log plots. The [[internet|Internet]] was both object and instrument: a network that could measure other networks. [[silk-road|The Silk Road]] is a reminder that the form is older than the plots. Goods, gods, and plagues were already traveling a graph.`,
    tension: `The fault is between a universal science of nets and a pile of analogies. Preferential attachment is not a social theory; a power law is not a destiny; visualization can make a sparse relation look like a cosmos. There is a political tension in hubs: networks that are robust to random failure are fragile to targeted attack, which is knowledge that interests both epidemiologists and tyrants. Privacy and power live in the same adjacency matrix. Another tension is the node. Treating a person, a paper, or a gene as a dot erases the interior that [[mind|Mind]] and [[biology|Biology]] exist to study. The atlas uses networks anyway, because without the dots there is no travel. It records the erasure as the price of the map, not as a discovery that interiors were never there.`,
  },
  "knowledge-representation": {
    lede: `[[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] asks how a mind or a machine stores what it knows so that the knowledge can be used, shared, and revised. A representation is not a souvenir. It is a structure that makes some inferences cheap and others almost impossible, which is why the choice of structure is a political and a cognitive act. [[language|Language]], logic, frames, semantic nets, embeddings, and [[databases|Databases]] are rival answers. [[cartography-of-knowledge|Cartography of Knowledge]] is the public face of the same problem; [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] is the workshop in which the answers are forced to run. [[bartlett|Frederic Bartlett]]'s schemas were already a psychological theory of representation, whether he used the phrase or not.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that knowing is not only having a trace; it is having a trace in a form that supports the next act. A list supports counting; a tree supports taxonomy; a graph supports spreading activation and the kind of travel this atlas is built for; a vector supports similarity and the kind of travel a language model is built for. Each form forgets. Logic forgets the body; embeddings forget the exception unless the exception was frequent; frames forget what will not fit the slots. [[category-theory|Category Theory]] offers a mathematics of what is preserved when the names change; [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] offers a mathematics of the links. In psychology, [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] is the question of how concepts, rules, and images are actually held — a question [[cognitive-psychology|Cognitive psychology]] never quite closed. In computing, it is the question of ontologies, knowledge graphs, and the dream of a semantic web that the [[internet|Internet]] both inspired and drowned. The topic's claim on the atlas is immediate: these articles are representations, and the filaments are a bet about which inferences should be cheap.`,
    lineage: `Aristotle's categories, Linnaeus's nested names, and the encyclopedists' trees are the deep past of storing the known. Twentieth-century [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] made the past a research program: McCarthy's advice taker, Quillian's semantic memory, Minsky's frames, Schank's scripts, description logics, and OWL. Each generation promised that a sufficiently good formalism would make common sense computable. Each generation met the world. Meanwhile psychology moved from dual-coding and propositional networks to distributed representations and [[neural-networks|Neural Networks]], which store by weights rather than by slots. [[databases|Databases]] industrialized a different cut: tables, queries, transactions, a memory that is accountable. The present is a hybrid — knowledge graphs beside embeddings, symbols beside statistics — that looks like a truce and may only be a pause. [[library-of-alexandria|The Library of Alexandria]] remains the emblem of the older hope: that gathering is enough. Representation is the reminder that gathering without form is a pile.`,
    tension: `The open quarrel is whether meaning can live in a formal structure at all. The symbol-grounding problem, the frame problem, and the stubborn mess of common sense suggest that a representation unhooked from a body and a world will always be a map of maps. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] treats that as a diagnosis; classical AI treats it as an engineering backlog. A second quarrel is political. Ontologies encode whose categories win; search rankings encode whose pages count as knowledge; this atlas encodes an editorial climate. A third is compression. The better a representation generalizes, the more it risks erasing the case that should have broken it. The atlas cannot leave this topic, because the atlas is an instance of it. The tension is not a neighbor. It is the floor.`,
  },
  "cartography-of-knowledge": {
    lede: `[[cartography-of-knowledge|Cartography of Knowledge]] is the craft of mapping what is known so that a mind can travel it without getting lost, and sometimes so that it can get lost usefully. A catalog is not a map; an alphabet is not a geography; a search box is not a horizon. The topic sits between [[knowledge-representation|Knowledge Representation]] and [[history|History]], because every map of knowing is also a record of what a culture thought was worth finding. [[library-of-alexandria|The Library of Alexandria]], the tree of Porphyry, d'Alembert's Discours préliminaire, Otlet's Mundaneum, and this vault are one lineage with different instruments. The [[knowledge-atlas|Knowledge Atlas]] is the local instance: a graph pretending to be a world.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that navigation is a form of understanding. To know where an idea sits — which continent, which neighbors, which tensions — is not a substitute for knowing the idea, but it is a condition of not mistaking it for its nearest rival. Maps of knowledge can be trees, circles, subway diagrams, citation nets, or force-directed graphs; each projection lies in a characteristic way. A tree hides lateral kinships; a circle pretends the center is natural; a graph makes the linked look more real than the unlinked. [[networks|Networks]] and [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] supply the present's favorite projection. [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] supplies a method of drawing: readers leave traces, traces become paths, paths become the map. The craft is therefore both editorial and collective. Someone chooses the hubs; many hands thicken the edges. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] is the hope of that thickening; canon formation is the risk. A good cartography of knowledge makes the risk visible as a legend on the map, not as a secret in the projection.`,
    lineage: `Ancient taxonomies and memory palaces were already maps of the known, built for a mind that had to carry its library. Medieval mappae mundi put Jerusalem at the center; early-modern cabinets of curiosity put the collector there; Enlightenment encyclopedias put the alphabet and, behind it, a tree of reason. Paul Otlet tried to index the world on cards; Vannevar Bush dreamed of a memex; Nelson named hypertext; the [[internet|Internet]] implemented a version that no cartographer controls. Citation analysis, from Garfield to modern scientometrics, made the literature a graph; [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]]'s hubs appeared there too. [[library-of-alexandria|The Library of Alexandria]] remains the caution: gathering without a durable map is a fire waiting for a date. The [[printing-press|Printing Press]] lowered the cost of copies and raised the cost of orientation, a trade this vault is still paying. The lineage arrives here as a question: after search, what is a map for. The answer this atlas gives is travel that can be argued with.`,
    tension: `Every map of knowledge conceals by choosing a center. This vault centers relation and eight climates; it therefore underplays chemistry as such, law, theology, and the arts as arts, which live here as weather inside other continents. That is an editorial fact, not a discovery about the world. A second tension is authority. A cartography that is easy to traverse can feel complete; completeness is the one promise an honest atlas must not make. A third is the reader's body. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] reminds us that a glowing graph is still a screen, and that some knowing happens only in a laboratory, a clinic, or a street. Use the map to arrive. Then leave it, or it will start to look like the territory, which is how atlases become religions.`,
  },
  "wiener": {
    lede: `[[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] named [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] and treated control and communication as one science, whether the steersman was an animal, a thermostat, or a gun. Born in 1894, a prodigy with a Harvard doctorate at eighteen, he spent the war on anti-aircraft predictors and came out with a theory of [[feedback|Feedback]] that would not stay in the war. Cybernetics (1948) made a field; The Human Use of Human Beings warned what the field could do. [[information-theory|Information theory]] was a neighbor, not a possession; [[shannon|Claude Shannon]] measured the bit, Wiener wanted the loop. The atlas files him in [[systems|Systems]] because the loop is still the continent's first tool.`,
    idea: `The work is a unification. A governor, a reflex, a market, and a conversation are all, on Wiener's telling, devices that steer by comparing an output to a goal and feeding the difference back. Negative [[feedback|Feedback]] stabilizes; positive feedback runs away; noise is the price of being in the world. From wartime prediction he took the mathematics of filtering and of purpose as a computable relation between past input and future act. Purpose, so treated, no longer required a ghost. It required a loop. That move licensed a generation to talk about minds, societies, and machines in the same diagrams, which was fertile and, he knew, dangerous. He opposed the militarization of the science he had helped to make, and he worried about labor replaced by automata without a politics to match. God and Golem, Inc. later asked what it means to play creator with self-reproducing machines, a question that still sits beside [[turing|Alan Turing]] and beside [[von-neumann|John von Neumann]]. The through-line is ethical as much as technical: a science of steering must say who holds the tiller.`,
    lineage: `The life is a century's shortcut. A prodigy educated at home, then at Tufts, Harvard, Cornell, and Cambridge, Wiener worked with Russell, Hardy, and later with the MIT that would become his habitat. Brownian motion, harmonic analysis, and the Wiener process are the pure-mathematical cargo; the war was the turning. At MIT, with Bigelow and others, he built the predictor, met Rosenblueth, and found in the physiology of the nervous system a cousin of the servo. The Macy conferences, from 1946, gathered [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]], McCulloch, Mead, von Neumann, and a rotating cast of people who would not stay in their departments. Cybernetics became a banner, then a fashion, then a word that companies put on doors. Wiener died in 1964, already watching the banner fray into computer science, control engineering, and a thousand popularizations. The fraying was a success by other names. The atlas keeps the person at the origin of the names.`,
    tension: `The afterlife is a dispute over how much to load onto a loop. Engineers kept the mathematics and dropped the metaphysics; humanists kept the metaphors and dropped the mathematics; second-order cybernetics claimed the observer and, some said, lost the bite. Wiener's own politics — anti-militarism, a wary humanism — sit uneasily with the defense and corporate uses of control theory. There is also a historical argument: how much of modern [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] is Wiener's child and how much is a different bet, on symbols and later on data, that did not need his tiller. The figure remains because the questions he made ordinary — what is purpose without a soul, what is communication without a speaker, who is steered — did not retire with the word cybernetics. They migrated into [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]], [[mind|Mind]], and every thermostat that does not know it is a philosophy.`,
  },
  "bateson": {
    lede: `[[bateson|Gregory Bateson]] was the anthropologist of the pattern that connects mind, nature, and madness. Born in 1904 into a Cambridge biological dynasty, he refused to stay in a department: ethnography in New Guinea and Bali, wartime intelligence, psychiatry in California, ecology in Hawaii, a late fame as the sage of [[systems|Systems]]. Steps to an Ecology of Mind (1972) is not a monograph. It is a cabinet of essays that treat schizophrenia, play, evolution, and [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] as one subject. Double bind, schismogenesis, and the difference that makes a difference are his working tools. The atlas files him here because he made [[mind|Mind]] an ecological fact, not a private room.`,
    idea: `The work is a method of seeing relations rather than things. Schismogenesis names the runaway of complementary or symmetrical behaviors — the dance that makes enemies, or drunks, or nations. The double bind names a communication in which every move is wrong, a theory of schizophrenia that was also a theory of family and of power. Play, in the famous essay, is the paradox of a signal that says it is not what it is, a logical type that animals and people share. Information, borrowed from [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] and from [[shannon|Claude Shannon]] and then humanized, becomes a difference that makes a difference — a definition that [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]] used to bind biology to meaning. Mind, in the late ecology, is not inside a skull. It is immanent in the loops that include body, tools, and woods. That claim meets [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] and [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] as cousins, and it meets [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] as a moral education: the creature that does not see the loop will cut the branch it sits on. [[ecology|Ecology]] is not a metaphor in these pages. It is the widest mind he would admit.`,
    lineage: `The father, William Bateson, coined genetics; the son spent a life arguing that the unit of [[evolution|Evolution]] is not the gene alone but the pattern in which the gene lives. Cambridge anthropology, then fieldwork with Margaret Mead — a marriage and a collaboration — produced Naven and Balinese Character, studies of ethos as much as of kinship. The Macy conferences put him in a room with [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] and McCulloch; he took [[feedback|Feedback]] home to psychiatry. Palo Alto, the Veterans Administration, and the double-bind work with Haley, Weakland, and Jackson made a school that family therapy still argues with. Later, at the Oceanic Institute and in California, he became a public teacher of ecology and of the errors of conscious purpose. He died in 1980 at the San Francisco Zen Center, a fact that his admirers over-read and his detractors under-read. The lineage is biology, ethnography, and cybernetics braided until the braid is the point.`,
    tension: `The afterlife splits between a visionary of relations and a writer whose evidence was often a story. The double bind did not survive as a sufficient theory of schizophrenia; it survived as a name for a kind of injury. Anthropologists sometimes find him too cybernetic, cyberneticians too literary, biologists too willing to let mind leak into woods. The leaking is the thesis. If one wants a mind that stays in the head, Bateson will look like a mystic; if one wants a science of the pattern that connects, he will look like a founder. An ecology of mind can become quietism, a counsel to see the system and not to strike. He knew the danger: conscious purpose as a short-circuit. The atlas keeps that short-circuit visible, because a pattern that cannot be argued with is just another bind.`,
  },
  "barabasi": {
    lede: `[[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]] showed that many real [[networks|Networks]] grow by preferential attachment, producing a few hubs and a long tail of leaves. Born in 1967 in Transylvania, trained as a physicist, he made the web, citation graphs, and metabolic maps into one statistical object: the [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Network]]. Linked (2002) is the public book; the 1999 Science paper with Albert is the fuse. [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] had classified graphs; he asked how they get that way, and why hubs make a net robust to accident and fragile to attack. The [[internet|Internet]] was his first famous specimen and remains the one a reader can feel.`,
    idea: `The work is a growth rule with consequences. Give a network a tendency for new nodes to attach to already well-connected ones — 'the rich get richer' — and a power-law degree distribution appears, with no characteristic scale. That distribution is not a curiosity. It predicts that random failures mostly hit leaves and that a targeted strike on hubs can shatter the component. Epidemics, rumors, and innovations travel differently on such a skeleton than on a random graph or a lattice. Later work on controllability, on network medicine, and on the science of success extended the same taste for data plus a simple generative story. The physicist’s habit is to look for universals; the risk is to find them. Barabási's claim on the atlas is that [[networks|Networks]] are not a metaphor for society or for biology. They are a measurable form that society and biology sometimes share, and the sharing is an empirical question, not a slogan. [[complexity|Complexity]] needed that stubbornness. So did a public that had been told, vaguely, that everything is connected.`,
    lineage: `The intellectual parents are Erdős–Rényi random graphs, Price's cumulative advantage in citations, Simon's Yule process, and the sudden availability of large electronic maps. A physicist of the 1990s could, for the first time, download a piece of the web and count. Notre Dame, then Northeastern and Harvard, were the institutional bases; the Barabási–Albert model was the portable tool. A parallel lineage in sociology already knew hubs as opinion leaders and weak ties as bridges; the new work gave those intuitions exponents and a robustness theorem. Critiques followed as fast as the citations: many claimed power laws are not; many networks are not scale-free once one is careful; growth is not only preferential. The critiques are part of the lineage. A science of networks that could not be wrong would not be a science. The figure stands at the moment when [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] met data at planetary scale and had to become a natural science as well as a mathematics.`,
    tension: `The arguments after Barabási are about ubiquity and about politics. If scale-free is a default, then inequality of degree is a law of growth rather than a choice, which is a comforting story for whoever already sits at a hub. If scale-free is a sometimes, then the interesting question is which growth rules a community actually wants. Measurement fights — how to fit a power law, how to handle finite size, what counts as a link — are not pedantry; they decide whether the universe of nets has a type. There is also the dual-use of fragility: knowing where the hubs are is public-health wisdom and a targeting manual. The atlas keeps him because the questions he forced — how a net grows, where it breaks — remain right even when the answers are less universal than hoped.`,
  },
  "maturana": {
    lede: `[[maturana|Humberto Maturana]] described living systems as self-producing unities, and in doing so gave [[biology|Biology]] a definition that is an organization rather than a list of stuff. Born in 1928 in Chile and trained in medicine and vision, he worked with Francisco Varela to name [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] in the early 1970s. A cell, on this telling, is a network of processes that produces the components that keep producing the network, bounded by a membrane the processes themselves make. Cognition, famously, is not a later luxury. It is the operation of that closure in a medium. [[origin-of-life|The Origin of Life]] and [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] both still argue in the vocabulary he helped to mint.`,
    idea: `The work is a shift from composition to organization. One can inventory [[dna|DNA]], proteins, and lipids and still miss the circularity that makes the inventory a living thing rather than a soup. Autopoietic organization is that circularity: production of components, production of the boundary, production of the producer. Structural coupling is how such a unity goes on in a medium without violating its closure — a dance, not an instruction from outside. From this, Maturana drew a radical corollary about knowing: an observer cannot step outside the closure; [[perception|Perception]] is not a picture of a pre-given world but a bringing-forth within a history of coupling. The Biology of Cognition (1970) and later popular books made the corollary a public philosophy, sometimes to the discomfort of laboratory colleagues. [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]] and enactivism took the corollary as a founding charter. Critics wanted more biochemistry and fewer pronouncements about truth. Both the charter and the complaint are part of the node.`,
    lineage: `Santiago, the University of Chile, a period at Harvard and MIT in the 1950s, and work on color vision and the anatomy of the frog's visual system form the scientific apprenticeship. The biology of seeing already suggested that the nervous system does not pipe the world in; it constructs a correlation. Back in Chile, with Varela, the question became: what is the organization that a seeing system, or any living system, must have. Autopoiesis and Cognition (1972/1980) is the joint monument. Second-order [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]], von Foerster, and the Macy afterlife provided a climate in which a biology of the observer could be heard. The Pinochet coup made politics inescapable; Maturana's later ethics of the biology of love is a response that some readers treasure and others find untestable. He died in 2021, having watched autopoiesis travel into sociology (Luhmann), into [[origin-of-life|Origin of Life]] research, and into every seminar that needed a word for self-making. Travel is not fidelity. The lineage includes the misreadings.`,
    tension: `The afterlife is a fight over scope. If only cells are autopoietic, the concept is sharp and perhaps too small. If societies, minds, and institutions are autopoietic, the concept is generous and perhaps too large. Maturana was often stricter than his adopters. A second fight is epistemological. A biology that makes the observer constitutive can sound like a denial of a world, which he denied he was denying. A third is mechanism: can one point to the processes in a particular cell and show the circularity as more than a restatement of metabolism. [[origin-of-life|Origin of Life]] work that takes autopoiesis as a criterion must still say how the circularity got started, a problem [[emergence|Emergence]] names without solving. The figure remains because his question — what organization counts as living — did not become obsolete when the word became fashionable.`,
  },
  "cybernetics": {
    lede: `[[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] is the mid-century science of steering, whether the steersman is an animal, a thermostat, a firm, or a state. The Greek kybernetes is the same root as government; [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] made the pun a research program in 1948. [[feedback|Feedback]], information, and purpose-as-loop are the working parts; the Macy conferences were the salon. [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] is the descendant that kept the loops and often dropped the servomechanisms; [[artificial-intelligence|Artificial Intelligence]] is the descendant that kept the machines and often dropped the biology. [[information-theory|Information theory]] was the neighboring arithmetic, [[shannon|Claude Shannon]]'s more than Wiener's.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that control and communication are one problem. To steer is to send messages into a world that talks back, and to adjust. A servo, a reflex, a conversation, and a policy are isomorphic once one is willing to see the isomorphism, which is the gift and the overreach. Negative feedback holds a goal; positive feedback explodes or crystallizes; the observer, in second-order cybernetics, is inside the loop and cannot pretend to a view from nowhere. Ashby's law of requisite variety said that a controller must be at least as various as the system it hopes to hold. That sentence is an engineering theorem and a political warning. The concept therefore binds [[computing|Computing]] to [[mind|Mind]] and to [[ecology|Ecology]]: a nervous system, a climate policy, and a thermostat are cousins in a way that shocked 1950s departmental maps and still shocks some. Cousins are not copies. Cybernetics at its best is the art of saying how far the kinship goes.`,
    lineage: `Governors, homing torpedoes, and the wartime predictor are the machine ancestors; Cannon's homeostasis and the reflex arc are the biological ones. Wiener, Rosenblueth, and Bigelow's 1943 paper on purpose named the kinship. The Macy meetings then staged it, with McCulloch and Pitts's nets, von Neumann's machines, [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]] and Mead's ethnography, and a running argument about analog versus digital. By the 1960s the word was everywhere and the field was splitting: control engineering, computer science, family therapy, management, art. Beer tried to cybernate an economy in Allende's Chile; von Foerster named the second order; the fashion faded in Anglo-American academe and persisted in other climates. The present revival, under other names — [[feedback|Feedback]] literacy, [[complexity|Complexity]], AI safety — is a return of the repressed. The atlas keeps the original name so the repression can be read rather than only repeated.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do, and perhaps must not do, is be both a precise engineering of loops and a universal solvent of mind, society, and life. When it is the first, it is indispensable and modest. When it is the second, it becomes a mood, and moods do not fail cleanly. Second-order cybernetics, by putting the observer in, solved a philosophical problem and created a methodological one: how to be wrong. There is a political tension in the root. A science of government can serve a helmsman who should not be obeyed. Wiener saw this; not every descendant did. The atlas files cybernetics as a concept rather than a closed science, because the closed science already lives in control theory, and the leftover is the question of steering as a human act.`,
  },
  "feedback": {
    lede: `[[feedback|Feedback]] is the move by which a system's outputs return as inputs — the trick that makes thermostats, markets, bodies, and minds. Without it there is only a chain; with it there is a loop, and a loop can hold a goal, hunt, oscillate, or run away. [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] made the loop a science; [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] made it a literacy; [[learning|Learning]] is what a loop looks like when the return rewrites the system that sent it. [[ecology|Ecology]] had been living inside feedback before the word was fashionable: predator and prey, canopy and soil, the long memory of a forest. The concept is small, almost a diagram, and it reorganizes whatever it touches.`,
    idea: `The working idea is circular causality. A cause that does not feel its effect is a fantasy of isolation; most interesting systems are not isolated. Negative feedback opposes drift and yields the appearance of purpose: the governor that holds a speed, the reflex that holds a posture, the conversation that holds a topic. Positive feedback amplifies: a microphone howls, a boom feeds a boom, a panic sells. Delays inside either loop can turn a stabilizer into an oscillator, which is why well-meant policy so often pumps the very stock it meant to calm. [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] treated the mathematics of this as filtering and prediction; later [[systems-thinking|Systems Thinking]] treated it as a moral of intervention. In [[mind|Mind]], feedback is how [[attention|Attention]] is guided by what it has already selected, how [[emotion|Emotion]] appraises the body it has just changed, how a speaker hears herself. A theory that uses only arrows one way has not yet met a creature or a climate.`,
    lineage: `Watt's centrifugal governor is the textbook ancestor; Maxwell wrote a paper on it; the thermostat is the domestic one. Cannon's homeostasis gave physiology a name for internal loops; the wartime servo gave engineering a mathematics. [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] and the Macy group then generalized until the word feedback could be said of a family and of a forest. Forrester's industrial dynamics made the loops quantitative for firms; Meadows made them civic. In psychology, knowledge of results, reinforcement, and predictive-processing error signals are feedback under other headings, meeting [[learning|Learning]] and the [[bayesian-brain|Bayesian Brain]]. The [[internet|Internet]] industrialised a new species: metrics that change the behavior they measure, instantly, at scale. The lineage is therefore a machine principle that would not stay in the machine. Each era rediscovers that the return path was the plot.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is say, by itself, which loops to close. More feedback is not always better; some systems need insulation, delay, or a broken circuit, which is why [[ethics|Ethics]] of surveillance and of metrics cannot be read off a diagram. There is a rhetorical danger: once everything is a loop, no one is an actor, and responsibility evaporates into structure. [[game-theory|Game Theory]] and law refuse that evaporation. A technical tension remains between continuous control and the discrete, delayed, noisy feedback of living and social systems, which do not look like textbooks. The atlas keeps the concept anyway, because without the loop the rest of [[systems|Systems]] is a pile of parts. With it, the pile can steer — and can be asked who is steering.`,
  },
  "scale-free-networks": {
    lede: `[[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] are graphs whose degrees follow a power law: a few hubs, many leaves, no typical node. The web, some citation nets, some metabolic maps, and many airport systems have been claimed as members; the claims are now more careful than they were in 1999. [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]] and Albert's generative story — growth plus preferential attachment — made the shape feel inevitable. [[networks|Networks]] in general need not look like this; random graphs do not. The concept matters because the shape is a fate for failure: robust to accident, fragile to a targeted hit on the hubs, a fact the [[internet|Internet]] and epidemiology both have to live with.`,
    idea: `The working idea is a distribution without a scale. In a bell-curved world one may speak of the average city, the average airport, the average paper; in a power-law world the average is a poor guide to the typical, and the typical is a poor guide to the important. Hubs carry the connectivity. Remove them and distances explode, components split, contagions die — or, depending on the dynamics, begin. Preferential attachment is the simplest growth rule that yields the shape: new nodes prefer the already rich. Other rules — fitness, copying, optimization of cost and time — can yield similar tails, which is why a power law is a clue, not a confession of mechanism. [[graph-theory|Graph Theory]] classifies the object; statistical physics estimates the exponent; domain science must still say what a node and a link are. The [[knowledge-atlas|Knowledge Atlas]] is itself hubby: eight continents and a center, a design that is an editorial preferential attachment. Seeing that is part of reading the map, not an accident of the drawing.`,
    lineage: `Zipf, Pareto, and Yule knew heavy tails before anyone counted the web. Price, in 1976, described cumulative advantage in citation networks and wrote down a model that the later physics would rediscover. The late-1990s data made rediscovery a sensation: the web's in-degree, actor collaborations, power grids — some of those examples have since been demoted. Clauset, Shalizi, and Newman taught the field to test a power law rather than to admire a straight line on log-log paper. The lineage is therefore a burst of universality, then a penitence of method, then a quieter empirical science. [[barabasi|Albert-László Barabási]] remains the public name of the burst; the penitence is how the concept earned the right to stay in the atlas. [[complexity|Complexity]] needed a statistic that was not an average. Scale-free was the first celebrity of that need.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is tell you, from a tail alone, how to live. A hubby net can be a library, a monopoly, an epidemic, or an atlas. If preferential attachment is treated as a law of nature, inequality of degree becomes weather rather than climate policy. If it is treated as a design choice, then one may build in aging, capacity limits, or random attachment on purpose — as some infrastructures do. Measurement remains a quarrel: finite size, binning, and the definition of a link can create and destroy scale-freeness. The atlas keeps the concept because the qualitative warning about hubs is sturdier than any particular exponent. Watch the hubs. They are where the net holds, and where it can be made to fail.`,
  },
  "autopoiesis": {
    lede: `[[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] names the organization of the living: a system that produces the parts that produce it, bounded by a border it makes. [[maturana|Humberto Maturana]] and Francisco Varela coined the word in the early 1970s so that a cell would not have to be defined by a list of molecules or by a mysterious vital spark. The circularity is the spark, demystified. [[origin-of-life|The Origin of Life]] becomes, on this telling, the origin of a loop, not the origin of a substance. [[cell-biology|Cell biology]] supplies the instance; [[emergence|Emergence]] supplies the philosophical neighborhood; critics supply the question of whether a word this proud is still a mechanism.`,
    idea: `The working idea is organizational closure. A living unity is a network of processes of production that: make the components of the network, make the boundary that distinguishes the network from a medium, and thereby make the conditions for the processes to go on. Metabolism plus membrane is the slogan; the slogan is stricter than it sounds, because a factory that makes cars does not make the factory. Structural coupling allows such a unity to dance with a medium without opening the organization to instruction from outside. Cognition, in the Santiago school, is that dance, which is why autopoiesis leaked into [[mind|Mind]] and into [[embodied-cognition|Embodied Cognition]]. Whether the leak was licensed depends on whether one allows nervous systems, or only cells, to be the paradigm. Luhmann allowed societies; many biologists did not. The atlas files the concept in [[systems|Systems]] because it is a claim about what a whole must be in order to be alive, not a catalog of the stuff the whole happens to contain. Stuff can be replaced. The circularity, if it holds, cannot.`,
    lineage: `Kant already said that an organism is both cause and effect of itself; Claude Bernard's milieu intérieur and Cannon's homeostasis are physiological ancestors. [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] supplied the taste for circularity; what it did not supply was a criterion of the living. Maturana's work on vision, then the collaboration with Varela in Santiago, produced Autopoiesis and Cognition against a Chilean and a Macy backdrop. Varela later carried the idea into enaction, immune networks, and neurophenomenology; Maturana carried it into an ethics of living together. [[origin-of-life|Origin of Life]] research — autopoietic vesicles, the metabolism-first versus replicator-first fights — took the criterion as a design spec. Sociology, via Luhmann, took it as a theory of communication systems that produce communication. Each adoption changed the word. The lineage is a stem cell that differentiated, sometimes past recognition.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is decide its own scope without a fight. Too narrow, and it restates that cells metabolize behind membranes. Too wide, and every self-maintaining bureaucracy becomes alive, which flatters institutions and insults [[biology|Biology]]. A second tension is origin: a loop explains persistence better than it explains beginning, so abiogenesis still needs a hill to climb, not only a circle to occupy. A third is knowledge. If the observer is always inside a closure, the meeting with a world that can prove one wrong becomes delicate — a delicacy [[empiricism|Empiricism]] will not grant for free. The atlas keeps the word because the question it holds is the right size: what must a system be, in its organization, to count as living. Fashion cannot retire that question. It can only rename it.`,
  },
  "collective-intelligence": {
    lede: `[[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] is what a group can know or do that its members, alone, cannot. A flock turns, a market prices, a jury hears, an encyclopedia writes itself, a crowd panics. The concept lives between [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] and [[networks|Networks]]: architecture decides whether the many correct the one or whether the one infects the many. [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] is a method of the knowing; [[democracy|Democracy]] is a bet that the method can be made legitimate. [[knowledge-atlas|The Knowledge Atlas]] is a small instance, hoping to be the first kind of crowd.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that intelligence can be a property of a system of agents, not only of an agent. Condorcet's jury theorem already knew that, under independence and a better-than-chance competence, votes accumulate toward truth. Surowiecki's popularization named the wisdom of crowds and, more usefully, the conditions: diversity, independence, decentralization, aggregation. Violate them and one has groupthink, cascades, polarization — the [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] of a crowd that has stopped being a jury. Insect societies, slime molds, and immune systems show that the agents need not be human and need not represent the problem in order to solve it. [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] is how they often do it: traces in the world as a shared [[memory|Memory]]. Human collectives add [[language|Language]], status, and the internet's instant [[feedback|Feedback]], which can implement the conditions or wreck them at scale. The concept is therefore an engineering of situations as much as a romance of the many. Design the channels, or the channels will design you.`,
    lineage: `Political philosophy has argued the wisdom of the many since Athens and has feared the many just as long. Galton's ox, Condorcet's theorem, and Durkheim's collective representations are modern ancestors; [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]] and Mead watched cultures as minds. The web made the argument operational: Wikipedia, open source, prediction markets, and the [[internet|Internet]]'s swarming both of facts and of lies. Honeybee democracy, ant algorithms, and swarm robotics supplied nonhuman laboratories. [[game-theory|Game Theory]] contributed the spoiler: if it is rational to copy the apparent majority, independence dies and the theorem dies with it. The lineage is a braid of [[democracy|Democracy]], entomology, and platform design, each warning the others that a crowd is not automatically a mind, and that architecture decides which it becomes. [[information-age|The Information Age]] is the present tense of that warning.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is guarantee that a given collective is intelligent rather than loud. Metrics of clicks will not tell you; consensus will not tell you; even accuracy on a past task will not tell you about the next. There is a political tension in who gets to aggregate. A feed, a vote, a price, and an editorial board are different machines of the many, with different failures. There is an ethical tension in using insect models for human institutions: pheromone is not speech, and speech carries rights. [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] keeps showing how fast independence fails; [[networks|Networks]] keep showing how hubs concentrate the failure. The atlas keeps the hope and the failure modes in the same article, because a romance of the swarm is how a swarm becomes a mob.`,
  },
  "game-theory": {
    lede: `[[game-theory|Game Theory]] is the mathematics of interdependent choice — cooperation, defection, and the rules of the game. A decision is not a solitary bet once another agent is in the payoff; the other agent's mind is part of the problem. [[von-neumann|John von Neumann]] and Morgenstern axiomatized the two-person zero-sum world; Nash extended equilibrium to the rest; evolutionary games let strategies fight without anyone thinking. [[decision-making|Decision Making]] is the psychological neighbor; [[ethics|Ethics]] is the moral one; [[evolution|Evolution]] is the biological workshop in which the math keeps winning. [[capitalism|Capitalism]] and the [[cold-war|Cold War]] were among its first unplanned laboratories.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that rationality, when it is mutual, can be a trap as easily as a solution. The prisoner's dilemma, chicken, coordination, and public goods games are not puzzles for a rainy afternoon; they are skeletons of trust, arms races, conventions, and climate. Nash equilibrium names a point at which no one would unilaterally deviate, which is not the same as a point anyone would be glad to occupy. Repeated games, reputation, and institutions are how agents sometimes crawl out. Evolutionary stable strategies, from Maynard Smith, show that [[natural-selection|Natural Selection]] can occupy the same mathematics without a calculating subject. Behavioral game theory, meeting [[kahneman|Daniel Kahneman]], shows that the calculating subject is not the one who usually shows up. The concept therefore cuts across [[mind|Mind]], [[biology|Biology]], and political economy. It is a language for situations in which my best move depends on my model of your model of me — a hall of mirrors that [[social-psychology|Social Psychology]] studies in the wild and that this math studies in the skeleton.`,
    lineage: `Card-table probability is old; the modern theory is mid-century. von Neumann's minimax, the 1944 Theory of Games and Economic Behavior, and the RAND culture of the early Cold War made strategy a formal object. Nash's 1950 equilibrium, later a popular film, is the hinge. Schelling added focal points and the terrifying clarity of a game that can be played with cities as chips. Axelrod's tournaments made tit-for-tat famous and made cooperation look easy for a decade; later work made it look fragile again. Maynard Smith and Price carried the apparatus into [[evolution|Evolution]]; economists carried it into mechanism design and auctions; political scientists carried it into [[democracy|Democracy]] and conflict. The lineage is a tool that never stayed in the toolbox. Each discipline that borrowed it found that the borrowing changed what counted as a move.`,
    tension: `What the theory cannot yet do is say, from the skeleton, which game people think they are in. Framing, narrative, and [[emotion|Emotion]] choose the payoffs before the math begins, which is why a laboratory dilemma and a marriage are not the same matrix. A second tension is normative. Equilibrium is a prediction under assumptions, not a recommendation, but the prestige of the math has often smuggled a recommendation: be a Nash agent, or be a sucker. [[ethics|Ethics]] and [[utilitarianism|Utilitarianism]] have both tried to annex the tool and have both been annexed by it. A third tension is evolutionary: a strategy that spreads is not thereby a strategy that should. The atlas keeps the concept because interdependence is real and deserves a language. It keeps the tension because a language of traps can become a love of traps.`,
  },
  "self-organization": {
    lede: `[[self-organization|Self-Organization]] is order that grows from local interactions without a conductor. A flock, a crystal, a city street, a termite mound, and a market can all look designed from a distance and be, up close, the weather of many small rules. [[emergence|Emergence]] is the name of the new level; self-organization is a claim about the work that produced it. [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] is one of the crafts; [[complexity|Complexity]] is the climate in which the claim became a science. [[cybernetics|Cybernetics]] already knew that a loop can hold a form; this concept asks how the form can arise when no one held it first.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that global pattern need not be globally stored. Local rules — align with neighbors, prefer the richer patch, deposit a trace, follow a gradient — suffice, under the right regime, to grow a structure that looks like a plan. The right regime often sits far from equilibrium, spending [[entropy|Entropy]] to keep the pattern, as Prigogine insisted. In biology, morphogenesis, flocking, and neural patterning are the parade cases; in society, informal norms and some urban fabrics. The concept is a rebuke to two habits: explaining order by a boss, and explaining order by a miracle. It is not a rebuke to every boss. Some patterns need a constitution, a genome, or an editor. [[autopoiesis|Autopoiesis]] is a stricter cousin: not only order, but order that makes the maker. [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] asks when the order computes; self-organization asks only that it appear and persist. The atlas needs both questions, because a vault of articles is a designed object that also hopes to thicken by use, which is to say, a hybrid.`,
    lineage: `Kant's organism, Faraday's fields, and Turing's morphogenesis paper are ancestors who did not use the brand. Ashby's self-organizing systems, von Foerster, and the Brussels school around Prigogine made the brand scientific in the postwar decades. Haken's synergetics, Kauffman's Boolean nets, and the Santa Fe repertoire of flocks, sandpiles, and artificial societies made it a visible school. Grassé's termites, via [[stigmergy|Stigmergy]], supplied the entomological proof that traces can be the conductor. [[evolution|Evolution]] is the longest-running demonstration that local differential reproduction yields design-like order, a demonstration [[darwin|Charles Darwin]] already had without the later jargon. The lineage is physics of open systems plus biology of many agents plus a standing temptation to apply the moral to politics. The temptation is part of the history, not a later abuse, and the atlas records it as such rather than pretending the science arrived innocent of politics.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is mark, in advance, which local rules will grow a city and which will grow a riot. After the fact, every pattern has a rule; that is not an explanation. A political tension follows. Self-organization can be praised as freedom from planners or blamed as a way to ignore the planners who already sit in the initial conditions — property, law, force. A scientific tension follows too: without a conservation law or a Lyapunov function, 'the system self-organized' is a caption, not a result. [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] offered one result; many claimed systems do not obey it. The atlas keeps the concept as a method of suspicion toward hidden conductors, including the editorial conductor of this map. Suspicion is not a proof that none was there.`,
  },
  "stigmergy": {
    lede: `[[stigmergy|Stigmergy]] is coordination through traces left in the environment, from termite mounds to Wikipedia. No meeting is required. An agent acts, the world holds the act as a mark, and the next agent reads the mark as a task. Grassé named the trick in termites; the [[internet|Internet]] rediscovered it as a way to write together without a conductor. [[self-organization|Self-Organization]] is the larger weather; [[collective-intelligence|Collective Intelligence]] is the hope that the traces compute something worth knowing. The [[knowledge-atlas|Knowledge Atlas]] is a stigmergic bet: that a path walked becomes a path offered.`,
    idea: `The working idea is that the environment can be a [[memory|Memory]] and a protocol. Pheromone on a trail, a chip of mud on a mound, a wiki stub, a well-worn footpath, a bug tracker: each is a sign that both records work and elicits more work of a related kind. Positive [[feedback|Feedback]] thickens successful traces; evaporation or decay prevents old traces from ruling forever. The elegance is the saving of communication. Agents need not model one another if they can model the marks. The danger is the same saving: a mark is not an argument, and a thickened path may be a rut. Human stigmergy adds [[language|Language]], so the mark can be a sentence, a vote, a price, a citation. [[networks|Networks]] of such marks become infrastructure. [[cartography-of-knowledge|Cartography of Knowledge]] is, at its most honest, the drawing of traces that other minds can continue, which is why this vault prefers wikilinks to a table of contents. A table of contents is a conductor. A wikilink is a pheromone with a bibliography.`,
    lineage: `Pierre-Paul Grassé, in 1959, needed a word for how termites coordinate nest-building without a nest-plan in any termite. Stigmergy — from stigma, mark, and ergon, work — was the word. Ant algorithms in computer science, swarm robotics, and the study of pedestrian trails made the mechanism portable. The web then supplied human cases at a scale Grassé could not have measured: open-source repositories, wikis, prediction markets, even the way a city is written by walking. [[bateson|Gregory Bateson]] would have recognized a mind immanent in the marks; [[wiener|Norbert Wiener]] would have recognized a loop. The lineage is entomology plus [[computing|Computing]] plus the old fact, known to drovers and librarians, that a trace is a kind of speech. [[library-of-alexandria|The Library of Alexandria]] gathered texts; stigmergy gathers the gathering, the underlining, the next needed page.`,
    tension: `What the concept cannot yet do is distinguish a wise trail from a cattle chute. Preferential attachment of attention — another name for walking where others walked — yields [[scale-free-networks|Scale-Free Networks]] of use, which can be a canon or a monopoly of the first comer. Moderation, decay, and random exploration are the usual counter-measures; each is a politics. There is also a dignity question. Coordination without meeting can look like freedom from bureaucracy or like a denial of voice: the mark does not argue, it merely lies there. [[democracy|Democracy]] needs speech as well as traces. The atlas uses stigmergy and admits the use. Read the bright filaments as pheromone. Then ask who was able to walk first, and whose walks were allowed to remain.`,
  },
};
